     1	# Chapter 9 — The Queue
     2	
     3	Lira put her name on the ladder on the twelfth of the month, two days inside the deadline, at the registrar's table on the rim of the Crown yard, and before she did it she read out every other name on the list to Cael from memory.
     4	
     5	She never once glanced at the page. She stood at the table with her hands behind her back and went down the list in the order the names had gone onto it, with a line for each: which hall, which Path, how they had walked up to the table and how they had walked away. Two hundred and nine Copper practitioners on the bluff, and something over sixty had signed by then, and she had been on the rim or in the tiers for every one of them.
     6	
     7	"Third on the list. Stone, fourth-year. Came up the steps like he was going to argue with somebody and then didn't." She did not pause. "Fourth. Current, second-year, a girl. Signed with her left hand and then wished she hadn't. Fifth—"
     8	
     9	"You watched all of them," said Cael.
    10	
    11	"I said I would." Lira did not look round. "Every name before mine is somebody I saw choose to be counted. Now I know who's on the board and how they feel about it." She reached the end and stopped. "Sixty-four. And me."
    12	
    13	The clerk at the registrar's table was a stooped young man with ink on both thumbs who had clearly been listening to all of it with growing alarm. He turned the book round for her.
    14	
