    19	He told her. It took him most of two minutes, and he told it in the voice of a man who had told it a good many times and expected to be argued with at the end. Until the brackets posted, near the middle of term, the yard ran practice hours on its open floors, with a faculty member at this table and every result written into the practice register, which lay beside the ladder book in black boards and could be read by anybody. But when the brackets were drawn, they were seeded from last season's closing order. A practitioner who had fought last season started from wherever last season had left them. A practitioner who had not, started at the foot.
    20	
    21	"And a transfer," said the clerk, "hasn't fought last season. Not here. Whatever a transfer's papers say about other floors, the book puts it low. It isn't personal. It's the order of the book."
