    44	
    45	She fought her first practice bout the next afternoon, and Cael was on the third tier to see it.
    46	
    47	The Crown yard ran differently on practice afternoons. The sunk floor was divided by chalk into three rings, and a faculty member sat at the registrar's table with the practice register open in front of him, and anybody on the ladder could put their name on a slate at the table's end and be paired with the next name of their bracket that was waiting. Bouts were short: first to three clean touches, with any exchange the instructor could not split called even and run again, and his word final. The tiers were never more than a quarter full. But they were never empty, either, and Cael had noticed in his first week that the same faces came back every afternoon, sitting in the same places, with notebooks.
    48	
    49	Lira was paired with a Stone Path fourth-year, Copper Rank Six, broad through the hips and thick through the neck. Cael had seen him fight twice. He fought the way Stone practitioners were taught to fight on every floor Cael had ever stood on: he stood his ground, and when a strike came he let the Path set his feet into the floor so that for a breath nothing in the world could move him, and then he struck back off that anchor with his whole weight behind it.
    50	
    51	It was a good way to fight. Against anybody who came straight at him, it was very nearly unanswerable.
