   223	"Cael." She looked at him with complete seriousness. "Every place I've ever been let in, I've read to the back wall. Do you understand? Every library I've ever been let into, I came to the end of. Dellenmoor. Greyvane. Prynn's, very nearly, and Prynn's was the best." She laid her palm on the document. "Today somebody gave me a place I can't come to the end of. Not in the time I've got. I don't think anybody's ever given me anything better."
   224	
   225	Lira had come in at the door partway through and stood leaning on the frame with her arms folded, listening. She did not come further in.
   226	
   227	"You argued them down," she said.
   228	
   229	"I read them their own charter." Karis looked round at her. "It isn't the same thing. Arguing makes people dig in. Reading them their own clause makes them look for the door."
   230	
   231	"I'll remember that." Lira said it lightly, but Cael saw her look at the document on the table for a moment longer than she needed to, at the three terms in Bracken's small upright hand, written down, initialled, unamendable by a bad week. Then she pushed off the doorframe. "Practice register's out. I'm eight and nil."
   232	
   233	"Eight?" said Brom, sitting up.
   234	
   235	"A Mire third-year. He tried to make the floor sticky." She was already going. "It didn't stick."
   236	
   237	Karis watched her go, and then turned back to Cael, and her face had gone careful again.
   238	
   239	"Three terms," she said. "One for me. Two for the work. And one for you, really, though it's got my name on it." She folded the document along its crease. "You'd better start keeping a list of everything I write on you, so if Bracken's cabinet ever burns down we'll know what was in it."
   240	
   241	"You're joking."
   242	
   243	"Half," said Karis. "Write the list anyway."

