     1	# Chapter 11 — Silence on the Skin
     2	
     3	The key was still on its nail.
     4	
     5	He had walked past it most days for a month, on the path behind the assessment wing that ran down to the low hall, and every time he had looked at it and every time he had left it alone. It hung beside the door on a square iron nail at the height of his shoulder, a plain black key on a loop of leather gone shiny with handling, and the door it belonged to was locked from the outside. He had written both facts down on his first morning on the bluff, and underlined them, and had not been able to think of a single reason for either.
     6	
     7	He had not touched it, either. That had been a rule of his own, made on the first morning without quite deciding to make it. A key left out on a nail was an invitation of some kind, and he did not take invitations he could not read. At Greyvane he had learned what it cost to walk through a door because it happened to be open, and he had learned it in front of a panel. So the key had hung there for five weeks, and he had walked past it, and looked at it, and the question had grown a little heavier every time, the way a stone in a boot grows heavier over a long day.
     8	
     9	On the thirty-third morning he asked the porter, who was sitting on an upturned pail outside the wash-house cleaning the Copper Crown's case-key with a rag and some ash.
    10	
    11	"Hers," the porter told the case-key in his hands. "The low hall's hers."
    12	
    13	"Whose?"
    14	
    15	"Lattice." The porter breathed on the key and rubbed. "There's one instructor teaches it on this hill, and nine students learn it, and that's her hall and nobody else's. Thirty-one years she's had it. She had the lock put on the outside the first winter." He held the key up to the light and squinted at it. "You want to go in, you take the key down off the nail. You knock. You wait. If she wants you, she opens it from the inside with her own key, and you hang hers back up. If she doesn't want you, she doesn't open it, and you hang hers back up anyway and go away."
    16	
    17	"Why the key outside, then? If she opens it from in?"
    18	
    19	The porter looked up at him for perhaps the first time since Cael had come to the bluff, briefly, with a kind of patient pity.
    20	
    21	"So you have to stop," he said. "So you've got a key in your hand and a door in front of you and you can't just walk through. Lattice builds things in the air, lad. Lines. You can't see them, mostly, unless the light's right. You walk into a hall where somebody's halfway through building one, you find out where it was with your face." He went back to his rag. "Took a first-year's front tooth, the winter before the lock. She's never forgiven herself. Nor him, for running."
    22	
    23	"Is she in my eight?" said Cael, before he could stop himself.
    24	
    25	"Your what?"
    26	
    27	"Nothing." It had come out without his leave. The porter was the third line on the standing list, and had no idea he was on it, and would have found it very odd to be told. "Thank you."
    28	
    29	The porter grunted and went back to his rag without asking what an eight might be. He had the incurious patience of a man who had carried every trunk on the bluff and had stopped wondering, years ago, what was in any of them.
    30	
    31	It stayed with Cael all morning.
    32	
    33	He had watched the Lattice pupils four times, always from too far off: twice in the north hall, where two of them sometimes worked at the far end under a high window, and twice from the Crown yard's tiers, during practice afternoons, when a Lattice third-year had fought on the Copper rings. Every time he had come away with a page he did not trust; at forty yards, Lattice looked like nobody doing anything. The practitioners moved very little. Their opponents moved a great deal, and then stopped moving, and stood looking puzzled, or stepped carefully to one side as if there were something in the way, and Cael could never see what. He had a page headed *Lattice* in the observation notebook with eleven lines under it, and every one of the eleven was a guess, and he had marked them all as guesses.
    34	
    35	He did not want a twelfth guess; he wanted to stand next to one.
    36	
    37	So at the change of bell after noon, when the halls emptied and filled again, he went down the path behind the assessment wing, took the key off the nail, and knocked.
    38	
    39	Nothing happened for long enough for him to begin turning the key over in his fingers, wondering whether this was a refusal. He did not hang it back. The porter had not said how long to wait, and a rule that did not say how long usually meant longer than you thought.
    40	
    41	Then the lock turned from the inside, and the door opened a hand's width, and an old woman looked out at him.
    42	
    43	---
    44	
    45	She was small and very upright, past sixty, in a grey wool dress with the sleeves pinned back above the elbow, and she had the stillest hands he had ever seen on anybody. They hung at her sides as if they had been set down there and told to wait. She looked at him, and at the key in his fingers, and at him again.
    46	
    47	"Hang it up," she said.
    48	
    49	He hung it on its nail, and the door swung wide.
    50	
    51	The low hall was one room, low-ceilinged as its name, with its windows set very high along one wall, so the light came down in long slanting bars and lay across the floor in stripes. The floor was plain stone, swept bare. Two pupils were standing in the middle of it, a fifth-year girl with her hair in a tight knot and a younger boy, and both of them had stopped exactly where they were when the knock came, and had not moved since. Along the back wall a row of pegs held nine grey practice smocks, one for each student of the Path on the bluff, with a name stitched over each peg in blue thread. Below them the stone had been scored with fine straight lines, dozens of them, crossing and recrossing, like the marks a joiner leaves on a bench where he has measured the same cut a thousand times.
    52	
    53	The boy looked at Cael with open curiosity and then, catching the instructor's eye, looked hastily at the floor.
    54	
    55	"You're the provision," said the Lattice instructor.
    56	
    57	"Yes, Magistra."
    58	
    59	"I don't take the title." She did not explain. "What do you want?"
    60	
    61	He had thought about how to answer that on the path down. He had thought about several answers that were true and short and did not say very much, and he had decided against all of them, because he did not think this woman would let him in on any of them.
    62	
    63	"I read pressure," he said. "On the skin. A pace and a half out, I can feel where a weight is and which way it's going, a fraction before it gets there. It works on everything I've ever stood near." He kept his eyes on hers. "I've never stood near Lattice. I'd like to, while somebody builds, if you'll allow it. I want to know what I feel."
    64	
    65	For a long moment her face gave him nothing whatever.
    66	
    67	"Why?"
    68	
    69	"Because if it doesn't work on Lattice, I'd rather learn that here," said Cael, "than on a floor where it matters."
    70	
    71	Something moved very slightly at the corner of her mouth.
    72	
    73	"Stand there," she said, and pointed with one still finger at a place on the stone a pace and a half from the fifth-year. "Do not move from it. Do not put out a hand. If you feel anything you want to tell me about, tell me afterward, not during." She turned to the girl. "Three points. Quarter speed. Count it so he can follow."
    74	
    75	The girl glanced at Cael once, without curiosity, and lifted her hands.
    76	
    77	She built slowly, as she had been told, and she counted under her breath while she did it, a low murmur like somebody reciting a lesson. "One, and set." Her right hand closed in the air a little above the height of her shoulder, as if she had taken hold of a thread hanging there. "Two, and set." Her left hand closed lower and farther out, almost level with Cael's forearm. "Draw." Between the two hands, for an instant, as she moved her head, he saw it: a line in the air, finer than a hair, catching one of the slanting bars of window light the way the strand of a web catches light in a hedge in the early morning, and then gone again when the light moved off it.
    78	
    79	"Three, and set."
    80	
    81	Her right foot slid forward a hand's width on the stone, and her right hand came down and closed at the height of her hip, and he knew without seeing it that the third line had joined the other two, because the air in the place in front of him had become, somehow, a place. He had no account of how he knew it. There was nothing to see. But the space between her three closed hands, eighteen inches from his own forearm, was no longer only air, and he would have staked a great deal on its being something he could not walk through.
    82	
    83	The read gave him nothing.
    84	
    85	It did not give him a little; it was not thin, the way it went thin at the edge of its pace and a half, or uncertain, the way it went uncertain when two weights arrived at once and he had to choose between them. It gave him exactly what it gave him standing alone in an empty corridor at night. Quiet. The ordinary quiet of his own forearms with nothing near them.
    86	
    87	A thing had been built eighteen inches from his skin that could have shut off two of his ways out, and the instrument he had trusted for three years above his own eyes had not stirred.
    88	
    89	He stood very still on his place on the stone, as he had been told, and found his mouth had gone dry. It was not fear of the frame. The frame was a lesson, built at quarter speed by a girl who did not care whether he lived or died, and it would go when she let it go. It was something colder and slower than fear, rising from underneath, the feeling of a man who has walked the same path in the dark every night for three years and has just been shown, by daylight, a well at the side of it that he never knew was there.
    90	
    91	---
    92	
    93	"Again," he said, when it was over. "Please. With my eyes shut."
    94	
    95	The instructor considered him.
    96	
    97	"Why shut?"
    98	
    99	"Because I saw the line. Once, in the light." He kept his voice level. "I want to know whether the read is quiet on its own, or whether I'm only noticing it's quiet because my eyes are louder."
   100	
   101	She nodded to the girl, and the girl let her frame go. It went without any sound or sign at all; he only knew it had gone because the place in front of him stopped being a place. Then she built it again, counting, and he stood with his eyes shut and his whole attention on the skin of his forearms and the back of his neck and the line of his jaw, where the read always came, and it did not come. One, and set. Two, and set. Draw. Three, and set. Nothing. He opened his eyes on a finished frame he still could not see and felt the hair stand up along his arms, not from anything the read had given him, but from the plain fact of its having given him nothing.
   102	
   103	"May I touch it?"
   104	
   105	"One finger," said the instructor. "Slowly. Where she tells you."
   106	
   107	The girl pointed with her chin at a place between her two upper hands. Cael put out his right forefinger, very slowly, toward empty air.
   108	
   109	It stopped.
   110	
   111	There had been no warning, and that was the thing. His finger was moving, and then it was not, and nothing had arrived on his skin before it stopped: no pressure building, no weight in the air, none of the early signs the read had always laid on him a fraction ahead of a strike. It was not like pushing against a wall, which pushed back. It was more like a sentence arriving at its full stop. His finger had simply been forbidden to go further, and was not going further, and that was all.
   112	
   113	"You're listening for a push," said the Lattice instructor.
   114	
   115	He looked round at her.
   116	
   117	"Every other Path on this bluff is a push of some kind. Force is a push. Blade is a push sharpened. Stone is a push that refuses to move." Her hands still hung quite motionless at her sides. "A frame doesn't push. It forbids. There is nothing moving in it for you to feel. You may as well listen for the weight of a law."
   118	
   119	He took his finger back; it tingled faintly, as if it had been somewhere it should not.
   120	
   121	He stood a while longer looking at the place where he knew the frame was, and then, because the read was still quiet and would go on being quiet, he did the only other thing he could think of. He let the frame go and read the girl.
   122	
   123	"Again," he said. "Once more. Please."
   124	
   125	She built it again, and this time he felt it. Not the frame. *Her.* As she said *one, and set*, her weight went forward over her right foot by the width of a finger, and the read laid that on his forearm, small and clear. On *two*, it went a little back and left. On *three*, as her foot slid forward on the stone, it went forward again and down. Each time she set a point, her body paid for it with a small shift of balance, and her body was a weight like any other weight, and the read could feel a weight.
   126	
   127	"Nothing from the frame," he said slowly. "But I can feel her setting it. Each point, she moves. Not much."
   128	
   129	The girl's face went faintly pink, which was the first thing it had done since he came in.
   130	
   131	"Yes," said the Lattice instructor. She sounded, if anything, mildly pleased with him, or perhaps with the girl. "Most of my pupils spend their first two years learning to set a point without moving anything else. Most of them never quite manage it." She glanced at the fifth-year. "She very nearly has."
   132	
   133	"She'll have it out of me by spring," said the girl, unexpectedly, in a dry low voice, to nobody in particular.
   134	
   135	"By spring," the instructor agreed. She turned back to Cael. "So you will be able to read her until spring, a little. You will be able to read the other eight a little longer than that. You will never read me." She said it without boasting, as a plain fact about the weather. "Hang the key on your way out."
   136	
   137	He thanked her. She inclined her head about the width of a finger, which he gathered was a great deal, and turned back to her pupils before he had reached the door.
   138	
   139	Outside, the noon light was very bright after the striped dimness of the low hall. He hung the key on its nail and stood with his hand still on the leather loop, looking at it. A month he had walked past it, and a month it had been hanging there, at the height of his shoulder, offering him the plainest piece of information on the whole bluff, and he had left it alone because he could not read the invitation. The invitation had been perfectly simple. *Stop. Knock. Wait.* Caution had hidden it from him.
   140	
   141	Behind the door he heard the girl begin to count again, low and steady. *One, and set.* He went back up the path before he could hear the second.
   142	
   143	---
   144	
   145	He wrote it that night at the desk by the window, and he put the frightening part first, so he could not soften it by getting there slowly.
   146	
   147	*Low hall, thirty-third day. The read and Lattice: nothing. I don't mean faint. I mean the quiet I get alone on a stair at night, while a girl built, twice, a thing eighteen inches from my arm that could have pinned me where I stood.*
   148	
   149	*Guess, unproven: the read only knows weight on the move. A frame is three held points and the shape between them. No weight travels in it. So there's nothing there for the read to find, and it finds nothing.*
   150	
   151	*And it said nothing about finding nothing. That's what I want in big letters.*
   152	
   153	He pressed harder than he needed to on the next part.
   154	
   155	*When a tool breaks with a noise, you learn where its edges are and keep your hands off them. When it breaks without one, it isn't broken. It's lying, and it lies in the same calm voice it uses for the truth. I've leaned on this one in the dark, in crowds, with my back to doors, for three years. Today it went blind to an entire Path and stayed perfectly calm about it.*
   156	
   157	*Nine people on this bluff build frames. And this hill teaches eleven Paths I've never been close to. Some of them it can't see. I won't know which until I'm beside them, and perhaps not even then, unless somebody tells me something's there.*
   158	
   159	*From today: the surface read's silence is not information. Every quiet is an unknown. Every floor, every time.*
   160	
   161	That last line held him a long while after he had sat back.
   162	
   163	Then he did something he had done perhaps four times since Denvash, each time for a thing he could not afford to be without. He took out the observation notebook, opened it to the Halcenvane section, and wrote the rule out again, word for word, on its own page. Whichever book he had in his hand from now on, the rule would be in it.
   164	
   165	*Correction, to try again: read the builder, not the build. The frame gives nothing, but the person setting it is a weight. Each point costs them a small shift of balance. A pupil still pays it. A master doesn't. So this works against the young and the unfinished, and against nobody else, and it gets worse every year they train.*
   166	
   167	*Price of the correction: reading her body tells me where she set each point, roughly, and when. What the points add up to, it can't say. Three points could be a wall, or a corner, or a door with one side open, and from her weight alone I can't tell which. So even against a pupil I'm reading the hands of a man who's writing, not the letter. Better than nothing. Much less than I'm used to.*
   168	
   169	*Two hidden things this month. The floor that was in my prices. The silence that was in my read. Both of them were in there all along, and I never looked, because I had no reason to think they were there.*
   170	
   171	*Go on looking for the third.*
   172	
   173	Sleep was a long time coming.
   174	
   175	He lay in the dark and went back, as he had not been able to stop himself going back, through every time in three years the read had been quiet and he had taken the quiet as clear. The back stair at the boarding house in Ardenmere, which he had climbed a hundred nights without a lamp because his forearms told him it was empty. The corridor outside the archive at Greyvane, the evening the Compact's officer had walked its length with his cases. The yard behind the residence wing, in the dark before the first bell, where he had turned and turned on frozen stone with his back to the gate. He had trusted all three silences, and every one of them, as it happened, had been empty. But he had not known it. He had only felt nothing, and called the nothing knowledge, and gone on.
   176	
   177	He thought, too, of the eleven supervisors and their eleven careful silences. That quiet, too, he had come close to filing as an answer. It was not one. A silence that had been arranged was not the same thing as a room with nobody in it, and he had been very close to forgetting the difference.
   178	
   179	*Rule, second half: the same goes for people.*
   180	
   181	He got up in the end, lit the lamp again, and wrote one more line under the rest, smaller, because he wanted it there in the morning when the fright had worn off and he would be tempted to think he had made too much of it.
   182	
   183	*It was empty every time. That's luck, not knowledge. Don't confuse the two again.*
   184	
   185	---
   186	
   187	On the thirty-fifth evening, five weeks from the gate to the day, the wall behind the second quadrangle held all four of them at dusk, each at some private work, and Cael had the binder open on the coping.
   188	
   189	At the far end, where an old hitching post stood up out of the turf, Brom was turning nothing at all, as slowly as he knew how. Rooke had given him the post two days before, and the instruction with it, and the instruction was simple and cruel. Brom was to turn nothing until he was certain it was real. He was to stand at the post, and Karis or Lira or Cael would come at him with feeds, honest ones and empty ones mixed, and he was to let every empty one go by without so much as a twitch, and turn only the honest ones. Everything he had built, it ran backward. Tonight it was Lira feeding, from in front of the post, her hands flat, and every third feed she sold was empty, and Brom turned on two of every three of them anyway, and groaned at himself, and went back to the post.
   190	
   191	"Worse," he said cheerfully, after one of them. "I'm definitely worse. Write that down."
   192	
   193	"I'm writing it down," said Cael.
   194	
   195	Cael had taken his turn at the feeds before supper and had found it harder than he expected. To sell Brom an empty strike he had to mean it nearly all the way, with his weight and his breath and his eyes, and then take it back; and Brom, who had known him three years, believed every one, because Cael had never once in three years lied to him on a floor. It had felt very strange to do it on purpose. Brom had not minded at all; *again*, he had said, every time, beaming. *That one got me properly.*
   196	
   197	Along the flat top of the wall Karis had laid out four documents from carrel eleven to catch the evening light, with a smooth stone on each corner against the river wind. She was reading the third, very slowly, with her finger on the line. Now and then a single word left her, addressed to nobody, and went into the margin.
   198	
   199	Lira gave Brom one more empty feed, watched him fail to let it go, and then came and leaned beside Cael on the coping with her eyes on the water. Down at the ferry landing the two grey coats were standing under their lamp. She looked at them for a while.
   200	
   201	"Ten past," she said. "They're late changing."
   202	
   203	"Are they?"
   204	
   205	"Ten minutes." Lira's eyes stayed on them. "First time in a month. Write that down too."
   206	
   207	He did. Then, because it had been sitting on him all day, he told Brom about the low hall.
   208	
   209	Brom came away from the post and listened with his wraps hanging loose from his hands. When Cael got to the part about the frame and the quiet, he frowned, and turned his right forearm over and looked at the inside of it, the way he looked at it after a fall.
   210	
   211	"Mine's the same, then," he said slowly. "If yours came from me. Mine'd be blind to her as well."
   212	
   213	"I don't know. I've never seen yours near a Lattice."
   214	
   215	"Nor have I." Brom considered his forearm with deep suspicion, as if it had been keeping something from him. "Three years I've been telling you it's the best thing I own." He wound the wrap back up. "I'll ask Rooke. He'll know. He knows everything and tells you a quarter of it."
   216	
   217	"Ask him what he does about it," said Cael.
   218	
   219	"I'll ask him that too." Brom went back to the post. "He'll say, *watch*. He says that about everything."
   220	
   221	There was no complaint in it. It sounded like a man quoting a cook whose bread had begun, against all his expectations, to rise.
   222	
   223	Cael bent over the binder.
   224	
   225	*Five weeks. Four of us, on four roads.*
   226	
   227	*Brom: a man warned him he'd be made worse, and is making him worse, exactly as promised, and I've never seen Brom so glad of anything.*
   228	
   229	*Lira: nine bouts, nine wins, in a register half the bluff reads with a pencil in hand, and still nothing in it can climb as high as her certificate.*
   230	
   231	*Karis: a key, a seam of law with no back wall, and three terms on paper that keep her work in a cabinet and off the shelves.*
   232	
   233	*Me: a named category, the finest boards I've stood on, eleven Paths still out of reach, a read caught lying this week, and no line anywhere.*
   234	
   235	It had taken five weeks to be able to put the next part down. He put it down.
   236	
   237	*Four of us, four doors, all real. A year ago none of us could have walked through any of them. Worth writing down on the days it doesn't feel like enough.*
   238	
   239	He closed the binder. Behind him, at the post, Brom turned on another empty feed, groaned, and laughed at himself, and Lira went back to give him the next.

