books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:3:The apparatus in hall three's north bay was called a resistance post, and Cael had priced it in his first fortnight as he priced everything, and had concluded that it was the best-designed object at Halcenvane and the one most likely to hurt somebody.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:21:The instructor — the one he had had in the standing eight for a fortnight in the autumn, a broad quiet man who taught Ash Path to first-years and supervised general floors because he liked the floors — was at the south end with two second-years and a rope frame.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:97:She took the whole north bay out of use on the spot, which Cael learned three days later had cost the academy the use of four resistance posts across two halls for eleven days and had produced, at the end of it, a replacement schedule for pins in every apparatus on campus. Rooke had apparently argued for the schedule in faculty session and Bracken had found the money in a fortnight, and Cael, hearing about it, had written down that the safest thing that had happened all term had happened because a pin broke in front of nine people rather than in front of one.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:151:Jask had seen somebody arrive who had been six feet away, and had asked the ordinary question, and had accepted an ordinary answer, and would tell the story eleven times over a fortnight in a version that got better each time and less accurate.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:153:And one person in that hall had watched a fifteen-year-old with a documented Copper-adjacent baseline cross six feet inside a third of a second and take a jammed Force release on a forearm and put it into a floor.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:171:*He had a clear line. He was at eleven meters, which is inside every range where his positional habit means he was looking at the whole bay because the whole bay was the interesting part of a room he was in. He has watched me train under supervision for eleven weeks and has a better baseline for what I do on a floor than Gault does, because Gault has six trials and he has eleven weeks.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:189:Seln drafted the exception report on the evening of the same day, at his own table, in his own room, in the fourth handwriting, with the window shut.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:195:Then he sat with the pen not touching the paper for a while, which was not deliberation, because there was nothing to deliberate. He had known the content of this report at the moment the force went into the floor plate. He had stood at the west wall with a stack of session forms against his chest and had watched a boy with a documented Copper-adjacent baseline cross six feet of hall in less time than the release took to leave the post, put a jammed Force delivery from a seventh-setting resistance core onto a forearm, and put it into the ground, and had understood every part of it, because he had spent fifteen years being the person in the room who understood what he was looking at.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:199:Reportable. Escalation-grade. Exception category two — *observed capability materially inconsistent with the subject's documented baseline* — which was the category the whole assignment had been constructed to fill, and which he had filled, in fifteen years, eleven times, and had been right on all eleven.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:203:*Subject assisted in a training-floor equipment incident, hall three, north bay, sixth bell, Third-day. Retaining pin failure in a resistance post caused an obstructed release during a Force Path enrollee's fourth declaration of a third set at the seventh setting. Subject was present at the bay edge and intervened. The Force Path enrollee was not struck. No injuries. Academy floor warden has taken four posts out of service pending a campus-wide pin replacement schedule; documentation attached. Response consistent with documented baseline.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:207:It was, sentence by sentence, true. The subject had assisted. The pin had failed. Nobody had been struck. There had been no injuries. The floor warden had taken the posts out of service and the schedule was attached and would, in fact, be the part of the report that an analyst three grades up would find useful, because analysts liked institutional weather and there was no institutional weather like an academy discovering a fault in nine years of apparatus a fortnight before an inspection nobody had announced yet.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:211:*Response consistent with documented baseline.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:215:An auditor reads for three things. The first is contradiction: does any sentence in the file argue with any other sentence in the file. This one did not. The subject's baseline document, filed by the academy's assessment office and copied into the monitoring product in the second week, recorded a Copper-adjacent practitioner with non-standard architecture, consistent execution, an honest four-repetition ceiling and a capability to receive force on a plate at a drop-frame's heaviest setting. A man reading that baseline and then reading *response consistent with documented baseline* against a floor incident in which nobody was hurt would find nothing to catch on. The baseline had force reception in it. The report said the response was consistent with the baseline. The two documents agreed.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-11.md:221:The interest was real. That was the part that made it work, and it was the part no untrained man ever understood about the craft: an omission survives audit because everything around it is genuine. He *was* interested in the pin. He had thought about the pin for an hour. A fatigue shear in a nine-year housing a fortnight before a charter inspection was the best piece of institutional weather he had recorded all term.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:19:She did it properly, which was the part he would remember afterward — not the finding, which he had been carrying the shape of for a fortnight without letting himself look at it, but the fact that she had built a whole apparatus of formality to hand it over inside, so that the person receiving it had somewhere to hold.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:21:The common room at the ninth bell. Door shut. Brom in the chair he had claimed in the first fortnight and never surrendered, one boot off, working a thumb into the arch of his foot. Lira on the window seat with her back against the frame and one knee up. Karis at the table with the ledger squared in front of her and a second, thinner notebook beside it that Cael had not seen before.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:47:"The stakes condition has never required combat. I want that on the record, because I'm the one who wrote the model and I know what I wrote. What the twenty-two nulls established is that intensity is not sufficient. What the match established is that *earnest engagement against him* is. The distinction is not violence. It's whether the other party is genuinely trying to accomplish something at his expense." She looked up. "Seln is running an intelligence operation against Cael's enrollment, on Compact instruction, at full professional economy, and has been for six weeks, and it is aimed at his file, his standing, his provision, and every door any of us walked through this year. There is nothing in this building more adversarial. There is nothing in his life more adversarial. A man in a bout wants to win an afternoon. This one is trying to answer the question that ends him."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:61:Not because of the finding. He had assembled the finding himself, in pieces, across a fortnight, and had recognized it at about the third piece and had then done the thing he did with conclusions he did not want: he had stopped adding pieces. It was, he thought — and he made himself think it plainly, because there was no point in a private honesty that only ran when it was comfortable — the exact failure the third column existed to prevent. *Discarded* was a column for hypotheses that the evidence had killed. It was not a column for hypotheses you had declined to test because you did not want the answer, and he had used it as one for fourteen days, and Karis had walked in with the answer anyway.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:81:"I consented," she said. "In writing. On terms I set. I chose the intensity, I chose the date, I told him what I wanted out of it, and when it worked I spent a fortnight finding out what it had cost me and I told him that too." She had her hands flat on the table. "Seln cannot consent. He will not be asked, because asking would end him — a covered officer who is told what his subject can do is an officer who now has to report it or become a liability, and either outcome destroys him. So the question isn't whether he'd agree. It's whether the impossibility of asking makes it worse or makes it moot, and I have been unable to decide that in six days, which for me is a very long time."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:187:He wrote that finding down, because it was the largest thing he had learned in a month and it was not about Seln at all.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-10.md:15:*Lira — Wind — Copper 2 — 6/0.* First line, first place, ninety names underneath it, the brass mark not beside it because the brass mark belonged to a champion and the season had six weeks left to run. Six bouts, six wins, a climb from twenty-second in eleven sessions that the yard had spent a fortnight arguing about and that Cael had reduced to arithmetic in about nine minutes: reach, every time, four places at a stroke, no wasted afternoons, the fastest legal ascent the ladder's mathematics permitted. She had done it exactly. She had not once taken a bout she did not need.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-10.md:63:*They've started building for her. That's the finding and it's four weeks earlier than I predicted. The cohort has a Lira plan now: don't chase the displacement, don't try to be where she lands, just refuse to commit until she's spent three bursts, then take the fourth landing beat.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-10.md:75:The opponent was a Copper Rank 7 from the cohort proper — one of Rooke's, a Blade Path third-year with a name Cael had known for a fortnight and a habit Cael had known for three days — and the bout was over in six exchanges, and every one of them ran the same paragraph Ephram had written in the third week of term.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-10.md:131:The Fiske bout was set for the fifteenth session and everybody in the building knew what it was three weeks before it happened.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-10.md:135:Actually it was neither of those things. Cael wrote what it actually was on the night before, in the observation notebook, on Fiske's page, under three weeks of notes.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-10.md:143:Seln was on the upper east tier, third of a cluster of six, in the shade.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:59:Lira had three bursts. Three, on that hip, today. The fourth was a thing she had told him she could not take, out loud, in hall one, in front of the man she was about to fight.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:211:Withrow presented it, because the chancellor presented the crowns, and she did it without one word of speech beyond the formula, which Cael thought was the best decision anybody made in that yard all day. Bracken read the season record — the same certified sheet, made that morning, before, when it could still have been either of them. Six wins, no losses, twenty-second to first in a hundred and thirty days, the fastest legal ascent the ladder's mathematics permitted.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:1:# Chapter 5 — Seln
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:5:Chairs made a man settle. Settling made him ask questions, and questions in a briefing room were a form of self-description — every one of them told the officer across the desk something about what its asker had already decided. Seln had worked out at twenty-four that the directorates preferred a man who took the paper, read it once, and left, and had spent the fifteen years since converting that preference into a currency he could actually spend. Ask nothing, and they sent you the assignments nobody wanted discussed. Those were usually the interesting ones, and always the ones with the least supervision.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:15:Seln read that twice and felt something close to professional approval, which he permitted himself because there was no one in the room qualified to notice it. Whoever had written *deliberate management rather than nervousness* had gone to the trouble of understanding the difference. Most of the analysts he'd fed in fifteen years could not have told the two apart on a good day, and had built entire careers on not needing to. A watched man who behaved is not the same animal as a watched man who behaves *at* you. The first is frightened. The second is talking, in a language with no words in it, and the only correct response is to stop standing where he can see you.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:19:The officer across the desk said something about the timeline, and something else about the reporting cadence — monthly product, quarterly summary, exception reports as warranted — and Seln listened to the parts that constrained him and let the rest go by. Then the officer said the sentence that briefing officers always eventually said, in the tone they always used, as though it were an insight they had brought with them into the room.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:23:"No," Seln agreed, which was not a concession, and gathered the pages.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:41:Shadow Path at Bronze-tier was not invisibility, whatever the folklore said, and Seln had spent a decade and a half being quietly grateful that the folklore said it. What the Path actually did, at his tier, was manage an observer's attention budget from the outside. It suppressed the small signals that made a human eye return to a place a second time — the flicker of motion at the edge of vision, the mismatch between a body and its background, the thousand tiny wrongnesses that a crowd's collective attention adds up and hands to somebody as a reason to look again. He did not vanish. He simply cost nothing to overlook.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:73:Halcenvane stood on a bluff above the river with the flat confidence of a place built on purpose, and Seln read it, on his first walk through, the only way he ever read anything.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:87:It was the dispersal after a closed panel session in the assessment wing: doors opening, four faculty and one enrollee coming out into the corridor's ordinary traffic, the boy last and in no hurry, carrying nothing, walking with a slight favoring in the left leg that Seln clocked and filed without deciding what it meant.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:89:Seln was thirty feet away with a stack of forms against his chest, and he did not look at him.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:97:Good, Seln thought, without feeling anything much about it. A famous subject is an easy subject. A hundred people watch him, so a hundred and one is not a number anybody counts.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:101:Cael's training-floor allocation came up for its second filing three days after the baseline demonstration, and he walked it over to the assessment wing himself rather than sending it through the residence clerk, because he wanted to see the assessment wing on an ordinary day.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:105:The wing was one long corridor, north end, with the apparatus rooms opening off the western side and the records rooms off the eastern, and it smelled of three things in a fixed proportion he could have written down after four minutes: lamp black, machine oil, and the dry mineral smell of chalk-plate dust. The oil was the apparatus. Halcenvane's assessment office measured people with instruments — resistance frames, load plates, timing glasses in brass cradles, a rack of graduated chalk-plates that recorded impact as a spread of cracks and were replaced after every use, which meant somebody in this building had a standing order for chalk-plates and a budget line to pay for it. At Greyvane, an assessment had been three faculty and their eyes. Here, three faculty sat behind an instrument and the instrument was the witness. He had understood on the day of the baseline what that changed, and it had taken him until now to phrase it: at Greyvane the panel decided what it had seen, and at Halcenvane the panel decided what the reading meant. One of those was easier to argue with. The other was harder to lie to.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:171:*Seln. Shadow. Bronze.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-05.md:183:The common room in the residence quadrangle was loud that night, because the ladder's opening brackets posted in three weeks and half the building already had opinions about the seeding.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-16.md:5:The Copper column had ninety-one names in it and by the season's last month it had four that mattered. Lira had gone from twenty-second to first in six wins inside eleven sessions, the fastest climb the ladder's mathematics permitted, and it had required six perfect afternoons in a row, and she had taken all six inside four exchanges. Fiske sat second, dethroned in the standings and undethroned in the crown, because the brass mark did not move until the final moved it. Brom stood third after a season that had cost him two losses and had given him back something Cael had watched arrive session by session: the seam was closing. Rooke's assignment had had a date on it and the date was the final, and by the last month Brom was converting absorption into counter with a stall Cael could still find from the gallery and could no longer find inside two exchanges.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-16.md:83:Cael's problem was a different shape and he spent three weeks failing to get it into fewer than three columns.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-16.md:87:*What the panel requires:* growth. That was the provision. The assay clause did not enroll a practitioner and then leave him alone; it enrolled him on a standing basis of evaluation by demonstration, and a demonstration that demonstrated nothing new was a demonstration that failed. Gault had set the baseline in the first month and had said the sentence that governed everything since — *baseline established; semester evaluation to measure against it* — and a boy who arrived in front of that panel exactly as capable as he had been in his first week would be, by the provision's own honest language, a boy whose enrollment had not been justified.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-16.md:93:The three columns did not conflict. That was the thing that took him three weeks to see and made him put the pen down when he saw it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-16.md:99:And then the part he had been circling for three weeks and had left to last, because it was the part with no column.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-16.md:143:*I've been calling it the calibration problem for three weeks. It isn't. It's a bout with a stranger where the stranger is me and the crowd doesn't get to know it's happening.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-16.md:165:Nothing moved. That was the whole of it and it was harder than it looked. The Crown final stayed on the nineteenth, scheduled in the third week of term by a chancellor who had not known a notice was coming and who now declined, publicly and without comment, to shift it out of a delegation's window. The semester evaluation stayed on the twentieth, on the calendar Gault had published before anybody had heard the word manifest, before the panel of record, at the standard set at the baseline. Rooke's cohort trained its ordinary sessions at its ordinary hours and turned in supervision signatures the same afternoon they were earned. The fire-watch went on logging lamp oil.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:1:# Chapter 12 — What Seln Keeps
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:3:Ten days after the pin sheared, Seln sat down at his own table in his own room with the shutter closed, and did a thing he had not done in nineteen postings.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:13:An officer who stops reporting and stops recording is worse than a liability. He is a fool. Because the hole is going to be found eventually — everything is found eventually, it is a slow apparatus but it is not a stupid one — and when it is found, the officer's entire defense rests on his ability to say what he knew and when he had known it, and a man who has kept nothing has no defense at all. He has only his word, and the word of a covered officer with a gap in his product is worth precisely nothing, and Seln had personally watched it be worth nothing twice.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:89:The assessment office's morning traffic ran between the second and third bells and was the most boring hour of Halcenvane's day, which was why Seln had arranged, in his first fortnight, to be the person who worked the counter through it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:95:They had not spoken in nine weeks. The last time had been a filing date, in the second week, and Cael had asked for it and Seln had given it and neither of them had said anything else. Since then there had been the schedule with the corrected transposition, and eleven weeks of a man standing at optimum sightlines, and a boy who could find him from a doorway in four seconds and had never once let his eye rest.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:97:Seln put the form on the counter, turned it round, and did not look up.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:103:"There's a coursework change going through for the term's back half," Seln said, to the form. "Field assessment. Gault is adding a unit."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:115:"I am," said Seln, and made a mark in the counter ledger. "It's a staff unit. Gault approves the syllabus and doesn't attend."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:141:"Enrolment sheet goes up on the residence board on the eleventh," said Seln, already reaching for the next form in the tray. "Put your name on it like everybody else."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:199:"Something went up. He filed *something* — a man in that office who filed nothing for a fortnight would be a man with a hole in his product, and he's not stupid." Cael turned the copy of his floor allocation over on the table and looked at the two dates and the two sets of initials. "Whatever went up didn't have the afternoon in it. That's the only reading the ground supports. Eleven days, no change, no bodies. He watched me stop a jammed seventh-setting release with a forearm and put it in the floor, and the apparatus that has spent two years and — I don't want to know how much — money watching me did not so much as add a man to a ferry landing."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:221:*He watched me stop Iron-tier force with a Copper baseline and his desk stayed quiet — the rotations outside haven't changed, which means nothing went up. Now he's handing me his own Path at teaching range. Either it's the best trap the Compact ever built, or the man they sent to watch me has started watching them. Karis says instruments don't change sides. Karis has never met this instrument.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-24.md:35:At the twenty-second minute the door opened and a clerk came out with a form and went upstairs and came back with a different form, and the four of them watched him do it with an intensity that made him visibly nervous, and Bracken said, "Ordinary," in the tone of a man who had spent twenty years learning which paper meant what and would have said something else if it had meant something else.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-24.md:37:At the fifty-first minute Lira came out.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-24.md:277:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. The oldest. Deployment reliable, working ceiling six repetitions as of the twentieth of Reaping, up from four at the baseline. Price: the landing beat, unshortened in three years of trying, and a hip that has now taken a full load in place of a floor once and does not want to do it again.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-24.md:281:*Iron-adjacent — Brom. Surface read. The cheapest thing I own and the one that saved a clerk from being landed on. Eleven of twelve on the drop-frame this year against five of six at the baseline, and eight of nine on the reserve frame after the fault — the last two degraded, which I said out loud at the time.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:99:Bouts ran on scheduled session afternoons, four to a session, matched by the assessment office. Challenges went up the column only, never more than four places above your own name; a win put you in the loser's place and pushed everyone between down a step. A loss cost nothing on the board — but a name that sat idle four sessions slid a place on its own, which meant the ladder's true engine was not winning. It was the obligation to keep fighting. Top four in each bracket at season's end seeded the semifinals, run in the last fortnight of the second term; the final fell on the last competition day of the year, witnessed, faculty-paneled, registrar-recorded.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:209:He collected the versions over the first fortnight because collecting them was the only thing he could think of to do with them, and by the end he had eleven distinct ones and had ranked them by distance from source, the way he ranked anything.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-08.md:67:Nyle stood ninth of ninety-one and had earned every place of it. Lira had opened the season twenty-second, because a transferred file with a review still sitting in a registry queue seeds low and there is no form for arguing about it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-08.md:89:The seam was the story, and it was no longer a private one. Brom's flaw had been named honestly at Greyvane a year ago and had cost him nothing there, because Greyvane had not held anybody fast enough to reach it: the self-taught redirect carried a gap between reading an incoming force and executing against it, and inside that gap he was, briefly, an ordinary person standing still. Ephram had found the gap in three sessions — Cael had watched most of the fortnight that followed from the east hall's gallery, nine pages' worth — and by now the entire cohort had been taught to look for it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-08.md:127:He went to every session. Not the ones Lira fought, or the ones Brom fought — every session, including practice hours, including the Silver bracket's thin column, where nineteen names produced maybe two bouts a fortnight, including the Iron bracket afternoons where Ephram held a court that was not quite a court.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-08.md:135:Which meant the seeding was not a starting position. Past a certain depth it was a ceiling. From twenty-second, the top was six wins away — six perfect afternoons, demanding but entirely available. From fiftieth it was thirteen, which a good season could still reach. From the high sixties the arithmetic simply closed — more wins required than bouts available — and it closed well before that for anyone who missed a single session, which everyone did. A quarter of the Copper column had been eliminated from the crown before the boards went up, by a scheduling constraint, and every one of them was down there fighting for it anyway.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-08.md:173:He wrote the season's opening entry in the Power Log on a Seventh-day night with the window open and the river going about its business below the bluff — the barge horns, the long call and the two short ones, which he had finally decoded three weeks ago and which meant *loaded, downstream, give way*, and which he now heard perhaps two hundred times a week and had stopped hearing at all.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:33:Karis set it out on the table in her own hand, on four sheets, and she had clearly been building it for a fortnight.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:43:"Five trials at the baseline, the same five tomorrow, and Gault will run them in the order he ran them then, because Gault runs everything in the same order. Trial one, the shutter-board: nine louvres, randomising drum, displacement on the signal — the pendulum takes your interval, the brass takes your distance. Two, the drop-frame with the surface read: a known weight from an unannounced rack position, off the marked square before it arrives. Three, the plate on its brace: received force, dispersal, repetition to your stated ceiling. Four, the frame again at its heaviest, the margin cut thin, and the chalked plate reading where your feet actually went rather than where you believe they went. Five, the first two again, cold, at the end, to find out what three hours have moved."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:47:"You know what they are. You don't know what they *say*, because you were the thing being measured and I've spent a fortnight reading them as an evaluator would." Karis put her finger on the first row. "Here's the number that matters and it's the only number I want you to carry into that room. At the baseline you took weight three on the drop-frame with a redirect the clerk logged as clean in five of six runs. Wind-adjacent displacement on the shutter-board: you volunteered a ceiling of four repetitions, unprompted, which Gault wrote into the record as the single most useful sentence anybody had said to him in that room all year. The read's contribution to the frame trial: five clean calls in six."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:51:"That's the question I've been on for a fortnight and here's the answer, and you're going to dislike the shape of it." She sat back. "A practitioner training four supervised sessions a week under competent instruction, from that baseline, with no unusual event, should present: weight four on the frame at the same cleanness, or weight three at six of six. Ceiling of five or six on the shutter-board, not four. That is the band. Above the band you are a new question and the whole room has to stop and consider what it's looking at. Below it and you have failed the provision's own honest language, because the enrollment was granted on a basis of evaluation by demonstration and a demonstration that demonstrates nothing is a demonstration that failed."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:63:"No." She said it with the certainty she used about four times a year. "That's the received wisdom and it's wrong and I've thought about it for a fortnight. Gault does not want effortless. Gault has told him twice, on the record, that he would rather have six identical trials at three-quarters than one extraordinary trial and five that disagree. Effortless is what an evaluator sees when a practitioner is holding something back and hoping the instrument won't notice."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:141:"This is the failure I hadn't predicted and it's the one I'd have missed," she said. "You've been thinking about the ceiling. The whole of your planning for a fortnight has been about not going *above* the band, because above the band is a new question and a new question is the thing that kills you. Yes?"
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:145:"Look at what you just gave me." She turned the sheet. "That's not restraint. That's regression. Your baseline interval consistency was better than that, and it's *in the record*, in Gault's own log, in the trial-four column, and any evaluator in the building can lay this morning's sheet against it in four seconds. If you go into that room tomorrow and hold everything by feel, you will not land at the bottom of the band. You'll land under it. And a practitioner who is measurably worse than he was at his baseline is not a boy who passed quietly. He's a boy whose enrollment produced negative growth on an instrument, in a term, under supervision, with the Compact in the room."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:223:*Panels see what they expect prepared for. Show growth where the baseline predicts it. Surprise them exactly once, small, early — a watcher who finds one thing stops hunting for a second.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:257:*Show growth where the baseline predicts it* was Karis's band, arrived at by a different road, which was the first thing that made him trust the slip: two people who had never spoken about it had built the same instruction out of opposite materials.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-20.md:263:And it was his to place. The provision measured what an enrollee elected to put on the floor — Gault had said so at the baseline, in those words — and an election could name its own place in the order.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-23.md:3:The compiled file came back to the assessment office on the twenty-fourth of Reaping, in the afternoon paper, with the delegation's return seal on the outer wrapper and a receipt slip attached, and Seln signed for it at the copying table with the senior clerk four feet away totalling the term's session ledger.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-23.md:13:Seln had audited product for two of his fifteen years, in a room with four other men who did nothing else, and he knew exactly what an unmarked stack meant, which was one of three things.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-23.md:147:"Since the training-floor incident I have counted the rotations outside the gate every third day," he said. "If a report had gone up with the actual thing in it, the rotations would have changed inside a fortnight, because that is what a resourced apparatus does with escalation-grade capability data, and I have watched it do exactly that twice. They didn't change. They haven't changed since. That went in the log before my birthday and I have never once been able to write down what it meant without it sounding like wanting something."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:5:Cael had expected some version of this. It had happened at Greyvane, where three tracks had pulled them apart along three different timetables and they had put the evenings back together by main force. What he had not expected was the direction of the asymmetry, and he wrote it down on the twenty-second day because it was the first thing about Halcenvane that had genuinely surprised him.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:51:Ephram found it in three sessions and then spent the next fortnight doing nothing else.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:157:The assay standing gave him supervised training access and evaluation-linked floor time. In practice that meant three things. He could train on any of the three halls' floors when a faculty supervisor was present and had signed for him — scheduled by the assessment office, rota'd across whichever instructor was on the floor, never the same one twice in a fortnight. He could attend any lecture. He could be present at anything the institution did in public.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:171:*The vantage point is real and it is better here than it was at Greyvane by a factor I can't yet estimate. Twenty-two declared Paths taught on these floors. I had charted eleven before I arrived, which means eleven more I had never seen at all — Lattice, Ash, Mire, and eight I've only had at a distance so far. Lattice alone would have been a year's work at Ardenmere and there are nine Lattice students here and one instructor who has taught it for thirty-one years.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:199:Then a Lattice Path pair took the neighboring floor, and Cael lost the rest of the session to watching them, and Lira let him, because she had lost sessions the same way herself.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:201:Lattice, close up, did not behave like anything he had charted.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:205:Lattice did not arrive as pressure. Lattice built *structure* — thin lines of held tension between two points, and then a third, and then the whole thing was a frame, and the frame did not push anything at all; it decided where his opponent was permitted to move next.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:213:*Iron-adjacent surface read against Lattice Path: no return. Not degraded. Absent.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:215:*Working explanation, unconfirmed: the read is a pressure instrument. It measures mass in motion. Lattice declarations don't move mass; they hold tension between fixed points and let the geometry do the work. There is nothing for the instrument to measure, so it measures nothing, and it does not tell me it's measuring nothing — it returns the same quiet it returns in an empty room.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-03.md:219:*There are nine Lattice students in this academy. There are eleven more Paths on these floors I have not yet had at close range. I have to assume the read is blind to some of them and I will not know which until I stand next to one.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:5:The assay provision's entry requirement was a demonstration before the assessment office. That much was in the document he had signed twice in Bracken's room. What the document did not spell out, and what took Cael three readings of the charter clause and one afternoon with Karis to extract, was the mechanism underneath it: the entry demonstration did not merely satisfy the requirement. It *established the baseline*. Every semester evaluation thereafter would be run against it. The provision did not ask him to be good. It asked him to be consistent, and then to grow, and it measured both against a line that he was about to draw himself, once, permanently, in a room with instruments in it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:13:"Two directions." Cael had been through both of them enough times to recite them. "I can set the baseline low — show less than I have, deliberately — and then every semester I can produce growth that isn't growth, just the rest of what I already had, released in instalments. That buys me years of comfortable evaluations."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:17:"It's very good, right up until it isn't." He turned the page around. "It has three prices. First, a low baseline is a document. It goes in the file and it says, in Gault's handwriting, what an assay-provision practitioner looked like on this date. That document is going to be read by an inspector eventually — Bracken has told me so twice, and Withrow told me in her first minute. A weak baseline is evidence that the provision admitted a weak practitioner, which is an argument against the provision itself, and the provision is the only thing I'm standing on."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:43:"Two. At full quality. Genuinely, at the top of what you can actually do with those two, with nothing shaved off." Lira sat back. "That's not a low baseline. That's a *true* baseline of the man in the transcript. And when you grow next semester, you grow honestly, in the two capabilities they already know about, and it'll be real growth, because you're fifteen and you're going to be better in five months whether you plan to be or not."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:59:*This is not the low baseline and it is not the whole truth. It is the honest maximum of the public version of me. If anybody ever asks me under oath whether the baseline was a fair statement of what the record showed, the answer is yes, and I will not have to think about it before I say it.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:83:Magister Gault was already seated. Stone Path, Silver-tier, somewhere in his fifties, a heavy man with a stillness to him that Cael read immediately as trained rather than temperamental. He did not stand. The two faculty seats were filled by an Ash Path instructor from the lecture complex, whom Cael had had in the standing eight for a fortnight, and a Mire Path instructor he had never seen before and who looked, on first read, like she resented the hour.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:91:"Three points of procedure. One: if at any moment you cannot do a thing, say so, and we will record that you could not do it, and it will not count against you in any way except that it will be true. A refusal I have to infer is worth less to me than a refusal you state. Two: I am measuring consistency, not maximum. I would rather have six identical trials at three-quarters of your capacity than one extraordinary trial and five that disagree with it. Three: this session establishes a baseline and the baseline is permanent. Do not perform for me. Perform for the person you will be in five months, who has to be better than whatever you write on my floor today."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-04.md:277:*He has not looked at me once in three weeks. That is not avoidance; I've watched him not-look at four other people. It's a policy. He does not spend attention on anything he hasn't decided is worth attention.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:159:Gault answered that in one paragraph, without warmth and without hesitation, and it was the longest speech Cael had heard him make in a full term. The clause required evaluation by demonstration before a panel of the institution, with the standard set by the institution and recorded. His office had set the standard at the baseline in the enrollee's first month, had published it, had calibrated the apparatus on its own schedule since, and would measure the enrollee against that standard on the twentieth whether or not anyone from the delegation chose to be in the room. "The clause does not tell me what standard to set," he said. "It tells me to set one, apply it, and write it down. I have. I do. I will."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:3:The first monthly product was due on the ninth, and Seln drafted it on the seventh, at his own table in his own room, in the fourth handwriting, with the window shut.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:25:Halcenvane's assessment wing was run by a man who did not care who you were. Magister Gault had asked Seln exactly four questions on the first morning — whether he could copy accurately, whether he could hold a calendar in his head, whether he understood that an evaluation date moved for injury and illness and nothing else, and whether he had any objection to working the hours the wing actually kept as opposed to the hours the charter said it kept. Seln had answered all four honestly, which had cost nothing, and had then been left alone in a way that would have been unimaginable at a dozen of his previous postings. Gault's whole administrative theory, as far as Seln could reconstruct it from a month of watching him operate, was that instruments do not have opinions and neither should the people who carry them. He did not chat. He did not ask where a man was from. He noticed errors within a day and said so once, flatly, and never mentioned them again.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:27:It was, from a tradecraft perspective, close to ideal. It was also — and Seln noted this the way he noted everything, without permitting it to become a preference — the first office in nineteen postings where nobody had asked him a single question he had needed to lie about.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:41:Second bell, hall three, and he had come along the upper walk with a stack of forms intending to take the north end of the rail, four bodies in from the stair, which was the position and had been the position for three weeks. The gallery had been empty. Not thin — empty, not one body on it, because the schedule said hall three did not run until the third bell that day and the schedule had been correct.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:49:*The schedule was correct.* The schedule said the third bell. The subject was in the hall at the second. Which meant the subject had come to hall three forty minutes outside his own allocation, without a filing, without a request, on a morning when nobody had any reason to be there — and had done it on the one day in three weeks when the gallery would therefore be empty.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:51:Seln had walked another forty paces before he let himself finish the thought, because he had learned early that a conclusion reached in motion sits better than one reached standing still.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:61:What he did about it was the interesting part, and Seln had spent the two weeks since being interested in it against his own better judgment.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:69:That was the finding, and Seln had checked it from four directions before he trusted it. No report to the chancellor — he would have known within a day, because Bracken's office copied everything to Gault's and he copied Gault's. No report to the assessment office. No complaint, no petition, no quiet word to an instructor. No change in routine: the training hours held, the lecture pattern held, the gallery attendance held, down to the same bench on the same side of the same tier at the Crown yard's ladder sessions. The associates behaved exactly as they had behaved on day one. The Wind Path girl, who by Seln's read was the least capable liar of the four and the most likely to give it away, had not so much as glanced at him with anything other than the ordinary indifference of an enrollee toward a member of staff — which, given what her face did about most things, meant somebody had asked her to manage it and she had agreed to.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:71:And underneath all of that steadiness, something had changed in the room's texture and it had taken Seln nine days to name it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:81:Seln thought about the file's question — *the Compact does not know what he is* — and about the four analysts and however many grades above them who had all agreed that this was the question, and about the twenty-eight days of product he had now filed against it, every line of which was true and none of which touched it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:109:That was the method he ended up building, and it was the best piece of work he had done since the Greyvane archive: he stopped measuring the man and started measuring where other people's eyes went. He already had the instrument — the glance count, six weeks of it now, a baseline for how often attention landed on a body in a Halcenvane corridor. So he ran it on the corridor instead of on himself, sampling one stretch of the covered walk at a fixed hour, counting how many transiting people marked *anyone* standing at the walk's east rail.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-07.md:153:That was the price, and it was not negotiable and never had been: the borrowed lightness returning to ordinary mass all at once, a jolt up through both heels, and half a breath of pure locked commitment in which he could not change direction if the building came down. He had used that half-breath correctly four hundred times. He used it wrong this time, because he was a beat late deciding what to do with it, because the surface read had come in late again, because the surface read had been coming in late for three weeks and he knew exactly why.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-13.md:167:He had written the terms out three weeks before, in the common room, with four people watching him do it, and had read them back so many times since that he did not need to remember them so much as fail to forget them.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-13.md:212:He had made an argument in a common room three weeks ago, out loud, to three people with every right to talk him out of it: that the mechanism's condition had never actually been violence; that in a year and a half of evidence what it required was *earnestness* — a person genuinely engaged against him, spending something real, meaning it — and that combat had only ever been the commonest way for a person to be that. Karis had said the conditions were met. Lira had said she distrusted how comfortable the sentence felt. Brom had said the thing that settled it. And Cael had written the line and had privately, in the part of himself he did not put on paper, expected to be wrong.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-13.md:272:"The provision text. Not your assessment record, not Gault's baseline — the *enrollment basis*. The clause, the citations, Bracken's verification chain." She let out a breath. "He was copying the argument, Cael. Somebody paid a man to find out exactly how the assay provision was built."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-13.md:312:*Five times before, somebody was hitting me. Tonight nobody touched me and the ledger told me more than it has ever told me. I made an argument three weeks ago about what this thing actually requires, and tonight the architecture agreed with the argument in a field it has never used before, and I am going to be honest on this page and say I do not like being agreed with by something I cannot ask a question.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-06.md:13:Roughly one in three, sliding slowly downward, which was a decay curve and would give him a half-life in another month. The number itself was not interesting. What was interesting was that he now had a baseline for what it cost to be looked at in this building, and a baseline is an instrument, and an instrument measures whatever you put in front of it, including things you were not looking for.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-06.md:93:At the thirty-second minute a man came along it with an armful of forms, going somewhere, and stopped at the north end four bodies in from the stair — except there were no bodies, because the gallery was empty, and so he did not stop there at all. He carried straight through and out the far door without breaking stride, and Cael understood, watching him not stop, that he had just learned more than the previous three weeks had taught him.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-06.md:135:He gave it to them the way he gave them everything now: the data first, the conclusion last, so that they could disagree with the conclusion without having to fight the data on the way. The glance count and its baseline. The thirty-one encounters and the zero. The two-layer map and the nineteen for nineteen. The empty gallery and the man who had walked on rather than stand alone on a good sightline. The brass lamp and the ten minutes.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-06.md:215:Counter-observation was not something a stranger did. It was something he did. It had prices — he had already logged the first of them, that afternoon, honestly, after a supervised session in hall three that had been the worst training hour he'd had since arriving. Three weeks of running a positional survey across an entire campus had cost him exactly what three weeks of intensive watching had always cost him, and he had known it would and had done it anyway: the low buzz behind the eyes that ran back into the skull, the attention arriving at the floor already half-spent. He had deployed the Wind-adjacent framework twice against a supervised drill and it had answered both times, clean, the way it always answered — hips dropping, the held half-breath, the half-body-width of displacement his legs had no honest right to produce, and then the landing beat, locked, half a breath of pure committed stillness at the end that a real opponent would someday learn to wait for. Both bursts had bought exactly what they always bought. The hip had complained down its leading edge until evening, which was the standing rent. The Iron-adjacent surface read had been the one that suffered — it was the quietest of the five and the one that ran on the same fuel as the watching, and by the session's end it had gone soft on him, giving him pressure information a full beat late, which in a real bout would have been a beat too late to use.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-14.md:181:The arithmetic was not complicated and he had been avoiding running it for a fortnight. Every other fragment he carried had been taken from somebody who knew afterward. Lira had known within days and had read the log itself, more of it than anyone. Brom had read the whole document in an alcove in Ardenmere and had then spent a day teaching Cael to use the piece of himself he'd lost. Karis had consented in advance, in her own handwriting, on terms she had drafted. Feryn had never known — but Feryn had been an opponent in a bout on a public circuit floor and had gone on with his life and had never once given Cael anything except an honest fight, and Reydan had asked to be told the answer someday and would be, someday, because he had asked.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-14.md:185:He had given him a Path to chart at operational range, which no academy floor on the continent would have shown him. He had corrected his paperwork. He had allocated him a sector on two consecutive nights and had made a noise on a stone floor so a boy in the dark would not be startled by a touch. He had watched a fifteen-year-old stop Iron-tier force with a Copper-adjacent baseline in a supervised hall, in front of a room, and whatever he had written about it — and Cael had spent eleven weeks watching the rotations outside the gate for the change that never came — he had not written *that*.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-22.md:45:"Tell the truth about everything the record already holds, and where he asks about something the record doesn't hold, say that I'm not going to answer, out loud, in words, and let it be logged." Cael turned the blank sheet over. "That's the change. Every other room I've been in, I've handled the thing I couldn't say by giving a true answer with a hole in it. Trial four, yesterday. Gault, at the baseline. The hearing. It's worked every time and it has cost me something every time and I've been writing the cost down for two years.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-22.md:115:"Second question." Not a pause; not a note; nothing. "What does the enrollee consider the weakest element of his baseline?"
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-22.md:153:"An archivist named Prynn taught me how the strata sit. A ledger-keeper named Vell taught me that a record is only worth what its worst entry is worth. Nobody taught me the schedule. I read it for three weeks and Karis read it with me and we found the seam because we were the only two people in the building who had a reason to read the whole thing instead of the part that applied to us."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-15.md:67:"My wing changes nothing," said Gault. It was the first time he had spoken. "The evaluation calendar stands as published. The instruments are calibrated on a schedule that has nothing to do with anybody's manifest and I will not have it said that they were calibrated because of one." He looked, briefly, at Cael. "The semester evaluation runs on its posted date, before the panel of record, under the standard I set at the baseline. If the delegation exercises its observation right, it observes that."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-15.md:173:The requisition reached the assessment office on the fourteenth day, and Seln had it in his hands before Gault did, because everything that arrived under seal came through the copying table and the copying table was his.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-15.md:187:*Subject assisted in a training-floor equipment incident. No injuries. Response consistent with documented baseline.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-15.md:205:He did not add anything. He did not remove anything. He did not go back into the incident report from the training floor and adjust the word *baseline*, which he had considered for perhaps four seconds on the day he wrote it and had not considered since. There was nothing in the compilation that was untrue and nothing in it that was complete, and he handed it across the counter to Gault's clerk at the fourth bell with the day's other paper, and it went into the wing's holding cabinet under seal to await a delegation that was five weeks out.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-15.md:227:They posted the inspection schedule on the covered walk's main board on the twenty-second day, and they posted it beside the Crown final's.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-15.md:241:This board had two dates on it and neither of them was a threat. One of them was a bout his best friends were going to fight each other in. The other was a procedure that the assay provision had required of him since the day he signed it, occurring on its posted date, before a panel of record, under a standard set at his own baseline — with a right of observation reserved by a delegation that had filed no question and made no demand and stated no business at all.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-17.md:19:*Why concede the category?* — beside session two, where the respondent, a boy of fifteen with no counsel and three weeks' preparation, had stood in front of a panel and given away, unforced, the single point everyone in the room had expected him to fight for.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-17.md:23:*Verify: three weeks' preparation — from what baseline?* — twice underlined, which for him was not emphasis but an instruction to himself, meaning *this is the load-bearing question and you will be tempted to accept the obvious answer.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-17.md:25:Three weeks was in the record. Three weeks was uncontested; the delegation itself had entered the date. Which meant that either the record was wrong about the three weeks, or something in the preceding two years had built a fifteen-year-old who could use three weeks like that, and neither the file nor the two years of monitoring summaries contained one sentence describing what.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-17.md:91:So he had dropped the useful one and kept the invisible one, and had done a Copper-baseline redirect drill at Copper baseline, fifty-four times, correctly, in front of people who had come four days up a river road to see what he was.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-17.md:197:It had not changed. Seln was at the copying table when Cael came in and at the copying table when he left, which was where a man was supposed to be, and there was a sealed compilation case on the shelf behind him with a wing index on its face that had gone out to the delegation on the thirteenth and come back on the fifteenth with a receipt stamp and no annotation of any kind.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-17.md:203:He turned that against the other thing he knew — that the reports themselves were true, complete in form, and contained approximately nothing, because he had watched a training-floor incident get filed as *response consistent with documented baseline* by a man who had been eleven meters away with a clear line — and got, for the first time all week, a genuine cold feeling in the back of his neck.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-17.md:223:*Seln operates. Position, economy, suppression rhythm — a craft, aimed, and I found it in six days and have spent six months learning it from the inside because he let me.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:89:Gault did not stand up. He had not stood up at the baseline and he did not stand up now, and Cael, who had spent five months learning the man, read the not-standing as the single most reassuring thing in the room: it meant the room was the same room.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:95:"Enrollee." Gault turned a page. "You know the format. The baseline's trials, in the baseline's order, questions between them, everything logged including the questions and the interval. Three points of procedure, and I will say them again, because I said them once in this room five months ago and a thing said once is a thing somebody can claim was never said.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:97:"One. If at any moment you cannot do a thing, say so, and we will record that you could not, and it will be true and it will not count against you. Two. I measure consistency, not maximum. Three." He looked up. "This session measures against the baseline you set in this room in the first month of term. It does not measure against anybody's expectation, anybody's transcript, or anything said in a corridor. If you have come here to be impressive, you have come to the wrong wing."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:101:"One matter remaining, then. The baseline measured what you elected to place in it. The rule holds at every sitting. Is there anything on your record you elect to add?"
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:137:"Trials one through seven," Gault said. "Displacement varies by less than half the width of one brass. Trial eight: same displacement, longer recovery, lateral error of one hand." He looked up. "At the baseline you volunteered a working ceiling of four repetitions, unprompted, before instrument confirmation. Restate it."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:143:"Six is free. The seventh costs me tomorrow's session and the eighth costs me the day after." Cael was breathing in a controlled way and made no attempt to hide that he was. "At the baseline four was free and six was over the ceiling. It's moved by two."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:149:Gault made a note that took him rather a long time. "For the record. Enrollee's stated working ceiling has advanced from four repetitions to six across the interval. Trials one to seven fall inside the baseline's variance and improve on its interval. The advance is stated by the enrollee before instrument confirmation, as at the baseline." He set the pen down. "That is what the provision is for," he said, to nobody in particular, in the driest voice Cael had ever heard him use, and turned the page.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:161:Gault gestured, and the clerk went to the north wall and brought over one of the two pieces of apparatus Cael had never been able to identify, in a first fortnight of looking or the six months since.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:169:"It measures applied heat. Water, sealed, with a column. Slow, honest, and impossible to argue with. The Ember and Ash cohorts are measured on it four times a year and it has never once flattered anybody." He turned a page. "The hearing record entered into evidence at Greyvane contains one demonstration exhibit describing an ignition-point capability, contact-adjacent, single channel, recorded late in that institution's term and recorded thinly. It is on your record. It was not on the floor at your baseline." He set the page down flat. "You have elected to place it now. Hand to the vessel. Raise the column."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:211:It happened in the interval after **trial three** — the combined trial, surface read and displacement, drop-frame release from an unannounced rack position, be off the marked square before the weight arrived. He had run it twelve times against the baseline's six and taken eleven of twelve, because the read had gotten better and the legs had gotten better and the two of them had spent five months learning to be one operation instead of two.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:261:Four times. By the fourth the forearm had gone dull to the elbow and the ache under the point of the shoulder had arrived exactly where it had arrived at the baseline and would last until the weekend exactly as it had then.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:267:Gault looked at the two sets of figures side by side — the baseline's and today's — and they were the same figures, to the graduation, on the same plate.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:269:"Trial four shows no advance against baseline. Dispersal low at both sittings, repetition ceiling four at both sittings, mechanism unchanged." He wrote it. "That is a flat line in a semester evaluation, and I am going to say the useful thing about it rather than the alarming one, because somebody in this building will otherwise say the alarming one for me. A practitioner who improves at everything simultaneously across five months is a practitioner whose evaluator should check his instruments. This wing measures six things. Five have moved. One has not. That is what a real semester looks like."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:285:The Mire Path instructor had checked the frame at the session's open. Cael had watched her do it — the trigger worked four times, the same as at the baseline five months ago when she had found the second detent dragging. She had nodded and sat down.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:361:The displacement had lost a hand's width and a fraction of interval, which beat the baseline's cold result by a margin the pendulum could see and a person could not. The read had lost nothing at all. It never lost anything. It was the cheapest thing he owned and the quietest, and that was — he had written this at Greyvane and had never had cause to revise it — the entire character of it and its entire limit.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:367:"Semester evaluation, assay-provision enrollee, measured against the baseline established in this room in the first month of term.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:369:"Growth against baseline: confirmed across all demonstrated capabilities. Working ceiling advanced by two repetitions, volunteered by the enrollee before instrument confirmation at both sittings. Combined-operation trials improved in both accuracy and interval. Ignition-point capability confirmed at this wing's standard against a prior thin record. One measured capability shows no advance and is logged as unchanged.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:413:"Growth against baseline confirmed. Consistency within tolerance at every trial. Requirement satisfied. Standing renewed."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:419:He wrote it at the window that night with the new pen, later than he meant to, with the hip packed and the shoulder aching in the place it had ached since the baseline session five months ago.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-21.md:437:*Twelve weeks old, an idle state that runs from waking, and not one flicker of it in three hours in front of the four best-placed pairs of eyes the Compact owns. Price: one logged interval and a headache. Pressure-adjacent didn't surface. Compression-adjacent didn't surface — I took four heavy weights into a joint that has a better answer available, and Gault wrote "no advance against baseline" about it and then defended me for it, which I did not deserve and cannot correct.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-01.md:107:"Twenty thousand, near enough," Karis said. "It's in the academy's own prospectus, so it's probably rounded up. Fourteen guild lodges, three of them with their own halls. Two registry offices — a district office and a stations office, which are not the same thing, and I intend to find out the difference within a fortnight."

