editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:3:Karis read the clause to him in carrel eleven with her finger under each word, the way Vell had once taught him to read a ledger, and on the fourth line she stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:He leaned over her shoulder. It was Halcenvane's own copy of the founding clause, with the charter article that carried it set out beneath, and below that the assessment office's standing rule for applying it, in a dry modern hand. He had read all three before, on the first night, and again in Bracken's room, and again on the night the notice came. He had not read them the way Karis read things, which was as if each word had been put there by somebody who might be lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:9:*The demonstration so made shall stand as the measure of record for the enrollee, and every subsequent demonstration shall be laid against it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:19:It was not the room he feared; he had stood before two hundred people with the Compact's warden at the other table and read a schedule back to them page by page, and no room of instruments could be worse than that. It was the arithmetic: every practitioner on every board had been set down by an Arbiter at fourteen and handed a number. He was being handed a pen, and whatever he drew with it on the day would go into a folder in Gault's cabinet and stay there. One day an inspector would take it out, with a grudge or only with a schedule, and read it on a table, or aloud in a hearing room. *Choose with that in mind,* Bracken had said, holding out the tag on its cord. Cael had nodded at the time as if he understood. He was only now beginning to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:33:"Two, it runs out." Another finger. "What I've held back is a fixed sum. Spend it as growth, a bit each semester, and one day I'll need to grow for real and find the purse empty. The requirement never stops. Every semester, no exceptions; that's the record I signed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:59:He could have recited it. Fourteen of Greyvane's monthly sittings had been read into the hearing as exhibits, one after another, and every one of them, under three assessors' names, had said the same two things about him: that he could get out of the way with the Wind, and that he could feel a weight coming before it came. A court had looked at those two and filed them where anybody could find them. Nothing else about how he was made had ever been put where the world could read it, except the Ember, and that only in a single late exhibit, half a page, so thin that showing it on Gault's floor would not confirm anything. It would announce something. Of Pressure and Compression it said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:79:*Baseline doctrine. Two capabilities are on the public record. Those two I show at full stretch, with nothing kept back inside either. Everything the record doesn't hold stays held.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:81:*Not the low line. Not the whole of me either. It's as much of the public boy as there is. Sworn, and asked if it was fair to the record: yes, at once, with no pause for a clerk to time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:89:*Every bit you've got, in those two.* He lay looking at the words in his head for a long while, and found he did not know how much that was. He knew his ceiling: four free bursts on timber now, and the fifth to pay. He knew the read's distance and its limits, and one new limit he had found in the low hall. But he had spent three years learning to show three-quarters of everything, on every floor, as a habit, the way a man who has been poor a long time goes on eating half his bread and saving the rest even after he has money. No panel had ever seen the whole of anything from him, and he was no longer sure he knew how to show it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:105:In the first, under a Shield instructor who crossed over at the eleventh minute, he ran the framework at its full pace against nobody, alone on the east hall's timber, and made himself go all the way through every burst instead of easing off at the end, and found he had been easing off at the end for so long it felt like falling. In the second, under a Mire Path instructor who had never supervised him and plainly resented the hour, he stood at the edge of the floor with his eyes shut while two second-years threw practice weights at the boards in front of him, and called the weight and the line of each before it landed. He was right about all of them. It was harder than it had any right to be, because he was trying, for the first time in years, to let the read be as good as it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:107:The Mire instructor watched the whole of the second session with her arms folded and her mouth turned down, and crossed over, like everybody, at about the fourteenth minute. But she did one thing none of the others had done. At the end, signing his sheet, she asked the second-years which weights they had thrown, and checked his calls against their answers, one by one, with her pen. They all matched, and she let that pass without a word. She only signed, and gave him the sheet, and went out. He wrote her into the margin of the eleven with a small mark beside her name: *checks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:111:*The assessment office will receive the entry demonstration of the assay-provision enrollee on the forty-eighth day, at the third bell, in the demonstration room of the assessment wing. Magister Gault presiding. The enrollee will present himself at the near door.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:113:He read it standing in the cloister, and found he was not afraid of it any longer. What he was instead had no name yet. It was a little like the moment on the mark before Edran, when he had found a question was steadier to stand on than an answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:"The Arbiter took about a minute and a half." Her eyes were on the wall's dark bulk, not on him. "It came, and I saw it, and it looked at me, and then the station clerk wrote *Copper* on a sheet and *Rank One*, and that was me. A minute and a half. Two years now I've been trying to get somebody to look longer than that." She was quiet a moment. "That man tomorrow has a room built for nothing but looking. Eleven instruments. He's got a clerk to time your blinks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:135:"An Arbiter station. One day. When the queue moves." She shrugged. "That's not the same. Same minute and a half, nicer manners."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:147:"I can't come in tomorrow," she said, sitting down on the step where Lira had been. "Closed panel. I asked Bracken. He said I could read the note afterward, when it's filed, like everybody else, and then he looked at me as if I'd asked to borrow his spectacles."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:179:Fifty feet by thirty, the room was floored in oak laid on bearers like the east hall's, and the boards answered his first step. Brass ran through the boards in a grid, finger-wide strips set flush a third of a pace apart, wall to wall in both directions. Nobody at the long table would need to stand to see where a foot had come down. He had grown up at Hesk's bench, watching an instrument-maker scribe a line on brass and check it three times before he cut, and he knew what it cost to set a true line over fifty feet, in any material at all. Whoever had laid this had done it by survey and not by eye. At the far end, where a careless setter's work always wandered, these ran true. The crossings were as sharp at the back wall as at his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:187:Magister Gault was already in the middle chair. He was a heavy man in his fifties, Silver-tier, with a stillness about him that Cael read at once as trained and not natural, the stillness of somebody who had decided long ago that moving was a thing to be done on purpose. He did not stand. On his right sat the Ash Path instructor from the lecture range, the fourth name on Cael's standing list, with her book against her chest. On his left sat the Mire instructor who had resented his last session, and who did not look as though she had grown to like the hour since. The clerk, a young man with a ledger and three pens laid in a row, had already written something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:197:"Three rules of this room. The first. When you reach something you cannot do, tell me. The book will record that you could not. Nothing more, and nothing held against you; a plain fact, written down. If I have to work out for myself that you've refused, it tells me less than if you had said so." His finger moved a little on the forms. "The second. I want sameness, not your best. Six runs that agree at three-quarters tell me more than a marvel followed by five that contradict it. The third." For the first time his eyes came straight to Cael's. "This morning is your line. You will be laid against it every half-year you are here. So don't do it for me. Do it for the boy who'll stand on this oak five months from now and have to beat whatever you leave here this morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:199:Of all the things Cael had readied himself for, he had not thought of this one: that the man holding the measure would name, in his first minute and as a matter of routine, the very thing about the measure that had kept Cael awake for three nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:7:It was generous. He understood that at once and was grateful for it. He could stand on any floor the office sent him to, under any instructor it named, and run whatever the record allowed; he could sit at the back of any lecture on the bluff, on any Path; he could be present at anything Halcenvane did in public, and Halcenvane did nearly everything in public. At Greyvane he had begged for half of this and been given a quarter. Here it came folded once, in a clerk's hand, without his asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:11:He found the hole on the twenty-eighth day, back in Lira's first week on the ladder, when her line in the practice register still held only three wins. He was at the registrar's table on the rim of the Crown yard, where he had gone to read Lira's line in the practice register and had stayed to read everything else. The ladder book lay open beside the register. He read the Copper column through, every name, two hundred and nine of them in last season's order and the new ones at the foot. Then he turned the page and read the Iron column, a hundred and thirty-eight, and then the short Silver column on the last page, and then he turned back to the beginning and stood with his hand flat on the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:27:"No," the clerk agreed sadly. "The margin never does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:43:*Also true, and better on paper than not: today I read all four hundred and twelve names in the ladder book, slowly, and mine isn't there and won't ever be, and I minded.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:45:*Not the crown. I couldn't care less about the crown. What I want is the line: a row on a wall in somebody else's chalk, saying what I did from a season's first bell to its last, for anybody passing to read and argue with.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:59:The slate had been Lira's idea, in the beginning, on the Ironyard boards. Paper tore and ink smeared, and a page could not be propped against a post at the edge of a floor and read from three paces while his breath was still short. A slate could. He had carried one in his pack ever since, through Ardenmere and Greyvane and down four days of road, wrapped in an old shirt so it would not crack, and he propped it now against the leg of the bench at the east hall's edge, a pace from the Ash instructor's feet. She glanced at it, and at the stub of chalk lying beside it, and went back to her book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:69:She came at him the way she had come at the Stone fourth-year, fast and low and straight, and he read her a pace and a half out on the skin of his forearms and slipped her on foot, without the Wind. She came again, and he slipped again. On the third she put down the short step he had watched her use on the yard, the half-step going nowhere, and he did not wait to see where it went; he held half a breath and let the framework take his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:83:They went again. He spent the second burst in the sixth minute, slipping her short step to the right, and the third in the eleventh, when she had stopped bothering with short steps and was simply faster than him on foot. Each time she called it; each time he wrote. The landings came down the same way, into the floor and back up softened. By the third his hip had begun to say something low and warm on the leading side, the way it did at the end of an evening on stone after two bursts, not three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:99:On the bench below the gallery the Ash instructor had put her book face down on her knee somewhere around the fourth burst. She was not reading it. She was watching him, quite openly, with an expression he could not read at all. When he glanced at her she lifted the book again and turned a page, and went on not reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:127:He turned back through the binder. Every price in it had been measured somewhere: the read on the Ironyard's planks, Compression on a stone floor in a cold hall, Ember on a training floor with Karis counting. Every one of them had been written down as though it were a fact about him, and every one had been taken in one place, under one set of conditions, and never taken again anywhere else, because he had never had anywhere else to take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:131:He lingered longest over Compression. Reydan's fragment did one thing: it took a push that arrived on him and sent it down through his bones into whatever he stood on. He had measured its price on a cold stone floor in a hall where the stone took everything he gave it and gave nothing back, a forearm ache delayed a minute and lasting two hours, a hand that shook. Oak on bearers did not only take; it gave, and then it returned. If he sent a push down through himself into oak on bearers, the oak would give under it. And then, if the east hall's boards did with a push what they had done with his landing, they would give some of it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:157:From the second week, he timed it. Somewhere between the tenth minute and the nineteenth it happened, and he could tie it to one thing only: the first time the Wind framework moved him faster than a walk. Their attention went over into a different state then, the way water goes over when it boils, and it stayed there. Books went down, and nobody glanced at the door again. They watched his feet, and the boards where his feet were going, until the bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:159:Each had a way of doing it, and he learned them apart by their ways. The Current lecturer, round and cheerful, hummed through the first eleven minutes and never hummed again after. A Shield man from the north hall wrote letters on his knee until the thirteenth, then folded the page and sat on it, as if he feared it might watch in his place. An old Stone master arrived with his eyes half closed and every sign of meaning to sleep; at the nineteenth minute both eyes came wide, and he leaned forward on his stick and stayed leaning, still as a post, to the end. Cael kept a line for each: the Path, the minute, the burst just before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:179:The Ash instructor had crossed over at the twelfth minute, like all the rest; he had the minute in the margin. But at the fourth burst, when her book had gone face down on her knee and she had been watching him with her whole face, she had seen him glance at her, and she had lifted the book again. It had not been embarrassment; he had watched a great many embarrassed people and knew the look. It had been a choice: she had wanted to stare and had decided, on purpose, in front of him, not to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:199:Brom lifted his head off the arm of the settle. "Why does that matter?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:201:"Because of the sweep." Karis sat at last. "Clause six of the committee's charter. When a cycle closes, whatever a student has made in the library gets swept up into the library's own holdings. Catalogued, shelved, opened to any reader on the bluff, or on the coast, or anybody who writes in with a fee." She tapped the third term. "Institutional work held by the registrar isn't. It stays in his cabinet, under his key, read by whoever he says may read it. It's a filing distinction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:207:Cael read the third term again. He thought about a delegation's boxes coming back up a hill under wax, a volume short, and an old woman at a high desk saying nothing about the gap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:211:"The committee hated it." Karis looked delighted. "They have a charter, you know. Eleven clauses. I'd read it four times before I went in. When the chair said the request was irregular, I read her clause nine back to her, the right of written appeal to the chancellor, and asked whether she'd like me to start writing. She said it wouldn't be necessary. Bracken was sitting at the end of the table the whole time looking at the ceiling." She stopped, and some of the glee went out of her, and something quieter came in behind it. "And there's the other thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:217:"How long to read them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:223:"Cael." She looked at him with complete seriousness. "Every place I've ever been let in, I've read to the back wall. Do you understand? Every library I've ever been let into, I came to the end of. Dellenmoor. Greyvane. Prynn's, very nearly, and Prynn's was the best." She laid her palm on the document. "Today somebody gave me a place I can't come to the end of. Not in the time I've got. I don't think anybody's ever given me anything better."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:229:"I read them their own charter." Karis looked round at her. "It isn't the same thing. Arguing makes people dig in. Reading them their own clause makes them look for the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:He had not touched it, either. That had been a rule of his own, made on the first morning without quite deciding to make it. A key left out on a nail was an invitation of some kind, and he did not take invitations he could not read. At Greyvane he had learned what it cost to walk through a door because it happened to be open, and he had learned it in front of a panel. So the key had hung there for five weeks, and he had walked past it, and looked at it, and the question had grown a little heavier every time, the way a stone in a boot grows heavier over a long day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:51:The low hall was one room, low-ceilinged as its name, with its windows set very high along one wall, so the light came down in long slanting bars and lay across the floor in stripes. The floor was plain stone, swept bare. Two pupils were standing in the middle of it, a fifth-year girl with her hair in a tight knot and a younger boy, and both of them had stopped exactly where they were when the knock came, and had not moved since. Along the back wall a row of pegs held nine grey practice smocks, one for each student of the Path on the bluff, with a name stitched over each peg in blue thread. Below them the stone had been scored with fine straight lines, dozens of them, crossing and recrossing, like the marks a joiner leaves on a bench where he has measured the same cut a thousand times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:63:"I read pressure," he said. "On the skin. A pace and a half out, I can feel where a weight is and which way it's going, a fraction before it gets there. It works on everything I've ever stood near." He kept his eyes on hers. "I've never stood near Lattice. I'd like to, while somebody builds, if you'll allow it. I want to know what I feel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:83:The read gave him nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:99:"Because I saw the line. Once, in the light." He kept his voice level. "I want to know whether the read is quiet on its own, or whether I'm only noticing it's quiet because my eyes are louder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:101:She nodded to the girl, and the girl let her frame go. It went without any sound or sign at all; he only knew it had gone because the place in front of him stopped being a place. Then she built it again, counting, and he stood with his eyes shut and his whole attention on the skin of his forearms and the back of his neck and the line of his jaw, where the read always came, and it did not come. One, and set. Two, and set. Draw. Three, and set. Nothing. He opened his eyes on a finished frame he still could not see and felt the hair stand up along his arms, not from anything the read had given him, but from the plain fact of its having given him nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:111:There had been no warning, and that was the thing. His finger was moving, and then it was not, and nothing had arrived on his skin before it stopped: no pressure building, no weight in the air, none of the early signs the read had always laid on him a fraction ahead of a strike. It was not like pushing against a wall, which pushed back. It was more like a sentence arriving at its full stop. His finger had simply been forbidden to go further, and was not going further, and that was all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:121:He stood a while longer looking at the place where he knew the frame was, and then, because the read was still quiet and would go on being quiet, he did the only other thing he could think of. He let the frame go and read the girl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:125:She built it again, and this time he felt it. Not the frame. *Her.* As she said *one, and set*, her weight went forward over her right foot by the width of a finger, and the read laid that on his forearm, small and clear. On *two*, it went a little back and left. On *three*, as her foot slid forward on the stone, it went forward again and down. Each time she set a point, her body paid for it with a small shift of balance, and her body was a weight like any other weight, and the read could feel a weight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:135:"By spring," the instructor agreed. She turned back to Cael. "So you will be able to read her until spring, a little. You will be able to read the other eight a little longer than that. You will never read me." She said it without boasting, as a plain fact about the weather. "Hang the key on your way out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:139:Outside, the noon light was very bright after the striped dimness of the low hall. He hung the key on its nail and stood with his hand still on the leather loop, looking at it. A month he had walked past it, and a month it had been hanging there, at the height of his shoulder, offering him the plainest piece of information on the whole bluff, and he had left it alone because he could not read the invitation. The invitation had been perfectly simple. *Stop. Knock. Wait.* Caution had hidden it from him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:147:*Low hall, thirty-third day. The read and Lattice: nothing. I don't mean faint. I mean the quiet I get alone on a stair at night, while a girl built, twice, a thing eighteen inches from my arm that could have pinned me where I stood.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:149:*Guess, unproven: the read only knows weight on the move. A frame is three held points and the shape between them. No weight travels in it. So there's nothing there for the read to find, and it finds nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:157:*Nine people on this bluff build frames. And this hill teaches eleven Paths I've never been close to. Some of them it can't see. I won't know which until I'm beside them, and perhaps not even then, unless somebody tells me something's there.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:159:*From today: the surface read's silence is not information. Every quiet is an unknown. Every floor, every time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:165:*Correction, to try again: read the builder, not the build. The frame gives nothing, but the person setting it is a weight. Each point costs them a small shift of balance. A pupil still pays it. A master doesn't. So this works against the young and the unfinished, and against nobody else, and it gets worse every year they train.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:169:*Two hidden things this month. The floor that was in my prices. The silence that was in my read. Both of them were in there all along, and I never looked, because I had no reason to think they were there.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:175:He lay in the dark and went back, as he had not been able to stop himself going back, through every time in three years the read had been quiet and he had taken the quiet as clear. The back stair at the boarding house in Ardenmere, which he had climbed a hundred nights without a lamp because his forearms told him it was empty. The corridor outside the archive at Greyvane, the evening the Compact's officer had walked its length with his cases. The yard behind the residence wing, in the dark before the first bell, where he had turned and turned on frozen stone with his back to the gate. He had trusted all three silences, and every one of them, as it happened, had been empty. But he had not known it. He had only felt nothing, and called the nothing knowledge, and gone on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:177:He thought, too, of the eleven supervisors and their eleven careful silences. That quiet, too, he had come close to filing as an answer. It was not one. A silence that had been arranged was not the same thing as a room with nobody in it, and he had been very close to forgetting the difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:197:Along the flat top of the wall Karis had laid out four documents from carrel eleven to catch the evening light, with a smooth stone on each corner against the river wind. She was reading the third, very slowly, with her finger on the line. Now and then a single word left her, addressed to nobody, and went into the margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:217:"Ask him what he does about it," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:233:*Me: a named category, the finest boards I've stood on, eleven Paths still out of reach, a read caught lying this week, and no line anywhere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:5:He worked out how before supper, because working out how was the only part of it he could do anything with. Nobody in the room had talked. He was sure of that by the evening and surer by the end of the week; he went at it four separate ways and found no road out of the demonstration room that any word could have taken. What had happened was simpler and, he thought, rather worse. By charter, the plain fact of any provision-status evaluation had to go up for the whole academy to read. It went on the board beside the theater door in the administration range, among the minutes of open sessions and every other act the institution performed. Halcenvane had kept its business in the open since its charter. So a little after the noon bell, the clerk had pinned up a slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:71:"I know it doesn't. I read it." Then, for the first time, Ephram truly looked at him: long, level, with no dislike anywhere in it. Edran's worst glare had weighed less. "Greyvane had nothing else to bet the house on, so they bet it on you. A place that badly needs a thing to be true can't see any more whether it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:87:"He's like that on the floor," he said at last. "That exact way. Looks at you as if you were a sum, and then does the sum out loud so the rest of the room can check his working." He picked at the strapping on his knuckles. "He's never once been unkind to me. He's never once been wrong about me, either. I can't decide which is harder to sit next to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:115:"Once," he said. "Second week. He'd told the cohort a girl's footwork was wrong, and two days later he stood up at the start of the hour and said he'd been wrong about it, and why, and sat down again. Rooke didn't say anything. Nobody did." He shrugged. "So he does. Only once it's true, that's all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:131:*He bought the primary source, read every page, worked out its exact reach, and gave me the answer with sixty people listening and nothing added to sweeten it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:139:"The Compact does not know what he is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:147:Seln had been standing the whole time, as he always did. A chair let a man settle, and a settled man began to wonder aloud, and in a room like this every wondering was a small admission of what the man had already made up his mind about. It was an old lesson. Those who handed out the work, he had found, were fondest of the officer who took his paper, read it once and left without a word, and he had let them be fond of him for it ever since. A man who never asked was the man they sent to places nobody wished to discuss. Those were the good places, mostly. They were also the ones nobody came to check.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:151:He had read the brief from the back, as he read every brief. Whoever had signed it sat three grades over the officer's head. Then the line the reports would climb: past that man, past his superior, to somewhere the officer had perhaps never been. Between them, those two facts set the limit on how honest the rest could afford to be. The front came last. Halfway down its second page, he had the drafter marked as a thinking man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:153:*Subject: enrollee, Halcenvane Academy, Ostrand; age fifteen; no classification of record. Greyvane: the challenge failed and the charter reading stands. Watch to date: open, funded, on rotation, without a break since the ruling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:159:He had read that twice. These days that was as close as the work came to pleasing him, and nobody in the room could have recognised the look on him. *Deliberate management rather than nervousness.* Somebody up there knew the difference. In all his years of passing product upward he had met perhaps three readers who could have told the two apart. Care in a watched man usually means fear. Care aimed *at* the watcher is speech without words, and the only sensible reply is silence, from a place he can't see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:201:He was thirty-eight years old. Somewhere in the last few postings his skill had outlived his wanting. There was nobody likely to read his product who could have done it better, and there was nothing he wanted any more from having done it well. He set that aside as he would have set aside a subject's quirk: noted, of no known use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:209:He read it as water reads a hillside. Where would people go, and where would they stop, and from where could they be seen stopping? Three halls on the north edge, each with two sets of doors: six streams that ran together for a quarter of an hour at every change of bell and then broke apart again. The assessment wing sat behind a low wall of its own: one gate, then two doors into the building. Excellent, if you worked inside. Very poor for anyone who wanted to stand outside and watch the gate. The lecture range, four floors high, a stair at either end and every landing open along its whole length, so that a man on one landing could be seen from the far stair by anybody who happened to be looking. The quadrangles built round courts, so many windows facing in that nobody ever looked out of any of them on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:219:Two instructors came out first, and a clerk with a folder, into the stream of people going by. Then, after a little, the boy came out alone, unhurried, carrying nothing, favouring the leading leg a little and holding his right arm close against his side the way a man holds an arm that has been asked to take something it did not care for. Behind him the magister had turned back into the room. Seln was perhaps thirty feet away. He stepped out of the stream, as a clerk does, to let it go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:227:Late that day, in the plain day-book a new assistant keeps and anybody may read, he noted one ordinary thing among the other ordinary things. The passage had been crowded at the change of bell, and the passage had been watching. Without looking he had counted about one in three of them turning a head as the boy passed, or checking a step, or weighing whether to nod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:233:Karis's census of the residence cards had reached the staff corridor by then, and she had made Cael promise to read every card on it the next time he passed that way, since it was on his road back from the north hall and not on hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:237:Each door on the bluff had its card, eye-high in a little frame of brass, giving name, Path and tier. Greyvane would never have hung such cards; it had been built on the suspicion that papers lied about people, and you do not fix to a door a figure you suspect. Halcenvane put the number on every door because here the number ran everything, the ladder and the brackets and the rooms and the seeding, and a corridor of cards was simply the ledger with its pages hung up where everybody walked. Cael's own card read *Cael. Assay provision. Evaluation by demonstration.*, in letters squeezed smaller than any other on the corridor so that the words would fit. Lira's read *Wind. Copper.* Brom's read *Iron Skin. Copper.*, and Brom tapped it with a knuckle every night on his way in, as if to remind it he was there. Karis's read *Ember. Iron.* It was the one card she said she would have bet her supper on, and she used it to measure all the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:243:In the end office the regular clerk sat frowning over a tally. Under the window, at the copying table, sat somebody Cael had not seen before. He took Cael's sheet without a word and read it right through, which most clerks never did. He turned it over to look at the authorizations on the back, checked both sets of initials in the corner, laid it on the right heap without glancing at the heaps, and wrote it into the big ledger with the two dates side by side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:25:He did it six times, and the clerk read six crossings and six intervals, and Gault's pen moved six times. Of the first three he felt no price at all. After the fourth there was something warm and low on the leading side, like a coal under ash. After the fifth the warmth had drawn itself into the thread he knew from the east hall, thin and bright, running from the crest of his hip down the inside of his leg to the knee: the bill being written out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:49:Gault turned his head an inch toward the clerk, whose pen was already lifted. "Put this down. Enrollee gives a working ceiling of four on sprung timber without being asked, and says what is wrong with the sixth before the instrument does." He turned back to Cael. "Since the brass went into this oak, nobody standing on it has said a more useful sentence. A man's best afternoon tells me very little. His honest ceiling tells me where to stand. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:57:So there would be nothing to watch. No feet, no shoulders, no breath, no face. Only a weight on a rail behind him and the question of where it was. Cael stood in the chalk square and looked at the blank wall of the room in front of him, and felt a small, plain gladness at how well the thing had been made. It was the read and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:59:He had never known how to describe the read to anybody who did not have it. The Wind was a thing he did. The read was more like a thing that happened to the weather on his skin. A weight on the move pressed on the air, and the air pressed on him, a little before the weight itself could, in a thin band along the outsides of his arms and up behind his ears. It told him how heavy and which way. It had never told him why, or what anybody hoped would happen next. It was cheap and silent and gave nothing away to anybody watching, and it would not reach one inch past a pace and a half however he strained it. One month, long ago, had gone to straining it further. Every month after, he had left it to be itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:63:He let himself remember that for exactly one breath. Then he set it down, because a frame was not a weight. What fell here would be a weight, and weights the read had found every single time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:75:Twelve times. He called each one as it came, and in the gaps the room went on making its noises round him: the pendulum, the drip at the end of the row into its brass cup, the clerk's nib. The ninth was wrong before it had finished arriving. It did not come all together. It came the way rain comes when the wind keeps changing its mind, a light early touch and then, a beat behind, the real weight, and he had two answers and had to pick one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:83:"It didn't arrive in one piece." After a moment Cael gave him the rest; it belonged on the record too. "A clean one is a single blow. This one arrived twice, a touch first and the weight after, and I had to choose which to believe. I think it let go slowly. That isn't an excuse. It's only what I felt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:85:The Mire instructor was already out of her chair. At the frame she worked the release by hand four times, slowly, her ear bent close, and at the fourth her thumb stopped on the pawl and stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:93:Gault wrote for a long time. The clerk wrote too, on a small separate slip, and pushed it into a slot on the frame's brass housing, where it stuck out like a little white tongue. *For service.* Cael looked at it over his shoulder and thought how easily a careful hand on a busy morning might pull a slip like that out to read it and forget to put it back, and the work never be done. Then he thought it was not his frame, nor his busy morning, and turned to face the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:95:The last three came clean. Eleven of twelve, the clerk read out, and the wrong one the ninth, and Cael stood in the chalk with his back to the rail and noticed, with some surprise, that he had begun to like the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:113:"No getting out of this one," said Gault. "Too close, too heavy. Catch it on the plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:119:He felt it the way a hand in the dark goes to a tool it has used before, already shaping to the grip. Since a bout he had lost fairly three years ago it had lived in him, used seldom, but always close under the skin. It did not keep a blow; it gathered it, turned it, and passed it on somewhere else. It was made for exactly this. If anybody in the room had been marking answers right and wrong, it was the right answer to the question in his hands, and the little dial would have swung round to a figure nobody at that table had seen in their lives.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:It was not on the record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:Then he lifted the top sheet. By charter the note had to be read in the enrollee's hearing. Gault read it, and Cael heard the words that would stand for him on this bluff for as long as he stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:He thought of the four pages of Karis's list, every line anybody had ever written about him: the station's word in brackets, the registry's forms, the observer track, the ruling, Bracken's record with its two signatures. Most of them had been written by people deciding what he was. A few by people taking great care not to. This one had been written by a man who had not tried at all, because deciding was no part of his work, and it was the plainest line on the list, and the only one, he thought, that would read exactly as true a year from now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:173:*The dear part is the man across the table. He was straight with me from the first minute, and I gave him something true with a hole in the middle of it, in his own room, for his own book, and he took it down and thanked me for being frank. I was frank. It still wasn't everything.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:177:*Quenna asked whether I could keep my hands still before people who'd enjoy seeing me hurt. That one's still open. This morning I held them still in front of a man who wants nothing from me but accuracy. I think, for the record, the second one was harder.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:187:"Half this faculty would give a good deal for one plain sentence about your nature." Gault said it flatly, as he had read out the crossings. "Some ask out of curiosity, some out of worry. Two of the worried ones, as I judge it, are worried for the school and not for their own skins, which is why they are worth your attention. You'll hear the question put a dozen ways before the term's out, give or take."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:191:"I expect you have." Gault moved the folder to his other arm. "I am not among the askers. I want you to know why. Take it for indifference and you'll misread me all year, and the work will go harder for us both." He looked at Cael directly, as he had in the first minute of the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:217:*Line drawn. Two capabilities, measured honestly, on the record for good. Four free on a floor that gives. Eleven in twelve; the miss belonged to the frame. Held, low. A shoulder till the week's out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:29:Lira was looking at him with an expression Cael could not read at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:37:From above, the floor looked like a pale lake. The boards ran the long way of the hall, oak, close-laid and polished by feet to a dull shine, and when the cohort came in and began to warm up he could feel it through the bench: a faint give, a faint return, as if the whole hall were breathing very slowly under them. He had written that down on his first morning on the bluff without knowing why it mattered. He wrote it again now, beside the date, with a line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:67:None of them ever explained, and none needed to. Each correction they made to him was written on his body in the one language every fighter on the floor could read, and he read it, and stored it, and was touched again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:73:Somewhere around the twentieth minute the lips stopped moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:91:He did not say anything. At the table Lira had spent most of the afternoon not filling in a ladder form; she raised her eyes to Cael, and he gave the smallest shake of his head. Karis put her pen down; the room's other occupants, two Iron girls arguing about a lecture, went on arguing about it. Brom sat with his hands on his knees and his eyes shut and his breathing slow and even, and the clock on the mantel ticked through most of a minute and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:Brom's turn needed a read; that was its nature and its strength: he let a strike commit, all the way, so that he knew precisely where its weight was going, and only then took it. But a read needed something to read, so Ephram gave him something. He sold Brom a strike with three-quarters of his weight behind it, honestly, enough to set the read going, and then took it back before the turn could close. The cost of that to Ephram was a sliver of a beat, a little lost balance he recovered as he stepped. The cost to Brom was the whole gap, every time: the full stretch between the read and the turn, spent on a strike that had never arrived. And Ephram could do it again at once, and again, because a Blade practitioner could recommit almost for nothing, and an Iron Skin turn could not be begun and abandoned without paying for the beginning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:169:Ephram came off the wall with his weight forward and stopped two paces out. He sold a strike at the left shoulder, hips turning, the back foot pushing honestly into the oak, and Brom's read took it; Cael saw Brom's shoulders begin to settle into the turn. Ephram was already gone from it. He had pulled the strike back into his own centre before it reached the halfway mark, and stepped a hand to the right, and sold another at the same shoulder from the new angle. Brom's read took that too, because it was honest weight again, and Brom was a man who believed honest weight. The third sale got no turn at all. Brom held, and held, and the strike did not come, and in the instant he let himself believe it would not come at all, it came: full, clean, a flat hand laid along his ribs on the side he had just stopped guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:173:Brom tried, on the eighth day, simply not to read. He stood under the feeds with his weight dead and let them go by, which was the obvious answer, and Ephram watched him do it for one exchange and then stopped feeding and struck. No read meant no turn, and without the turn there was nothing left of Brom but size. He accepted the touch with a slow nod, as though he had seen it coming, and went back to reading. He did not know another way to stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:201:"I wanted it where I'd feel it," said Brom, a little defensively. "Every time I bind up." He read it off the cloth, slowly, in Rooke's flat rhythm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:221:"He said so." Brom smiled faintly at the river. "A season, he told me, and I don't think Rooke says anything loosely." He straightened up off the coping, and stretched, and his shoulder cracked loudly in the dusk. "Keep your page, though. In a year I'd like to read it and see how stupid I looked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:3:Lira put her name on the ladder on the twelfth of the month, two days inside the deadline, at the registrar's table on the rim of the Crown yard, and before she did it she read out every other name on the list to Cael from memory.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:19:He told her. It took him most of two minutes, and he told it in the voice of a man who had told it a good many times and expected to be argued with at the end. Until the brackets posted, near the middle of term, the yard ran practice hours on its open floors, with a faculty member at this table and every result written into the practice register, which lay beside the ladder book in black boards and could be read by anybody. But when the brackets were drawn, they were seeded from last season's closing order. A practitioner who had fought last season started from wherever last season had left them. A practitioner who had not, started at the foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:33:Cael watched her read it. She went down the Copper column slowly, lips moving, and he knew what she was doing because he was doing it himself beside her. Last season's order stood at the top of the column; the new names would be added beneath it in the order of the book; the bottom was the sixty-fifth line, and the sixty-fifth line was hers. Between a session's paired bouts a practitioner could call out anybody above her in her own column, four names up and no further, and if she won, the place was hers and everybody she had passed slipped down one. Four rungs at a time, then, on a ladder of better than two hundred. Fewer than that, once the column settled, because a great many of the names below the seeded few would never climb at all. But even if she won every challenge she was allowed, in every window the board marked in red, she would need most of the season simply to arrive among the people she meant to beat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:63:She came at him again, just as fast and straight. He set his feet again, but this time, a stride out, she put her lead foot down short, a half-step that went nowhere, and the Wind did not carry her forward from it but sideways, low and quick along the chalk. The anchor had already taken, and the Stone fourth-year was planted, with all the weight of the world in his heels and his strike gathering. Where she had been there was only air; she was at his shoulder, and her flat hand was on the back of it, and he had not been able to turn because the Path that would have held him against anything had also held him still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:67:He was quick to learn, Cael gave him that. On the third exchange he did not anchor at all; he met her coming in with his feet free and his hands up, and tried to fight her as an ordinary large man fights a small fast one. It was the right answer to what she had just done. It was the wrong answer to Lira, who had spent two years on the Ardenmere circuit being chased by large men with free feet and had never once been caught by one. She went past him on the left without a touch, so close her sleeve brushed his, and came back round behind him and touched him between the shoulder blades on the way through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:77:At the table the instructor dipped his pen and wrote the line in the practice register, and Cael went down afterward to read it. *Lira, Wind, Copper Two (transfer), over* — the fourth-year's name — *Stone, Copper Six. Three to one. Practice.* In the margin, in the same hand, a small mark like a tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:93:One evening the porter had leaned his broom on the rail beside Cael and read the register across his shoulder, in no hurry at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:107:Lira read the books every evening. She had stopped saying anything about them after the first week, which Cael thought was worse, and she would stand at the board on the rim with her arms folded and her face set, and then walk away. The thirty-fourth evening, she stayed, long enough for him to come up beside her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:117:"And how many does the whole queue move?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:147:The Rank Four came out hard, as Cael had seen half the Copper board come out against her in the register's history, gathering his force early and throwing it at her to have it done before she settled. For the first minute she gave ground, and not gracefully. She stepped back badly twice, once nearly off her chalk, and a murmur went along the rim; Cael saw the Rank Four's shoulders lift, a man who has just begun to believe something. Then, at about the minute and a half, Fiske stopped giving ground. She did not seem to do anything in particular. She set herself a little off his line, and when he gathered and came, the force he met was not in front of him at all but beside his leading foot, a short flat push into the floor where his foot was about to land. He landed on it, and it was like stepping onto a stair somebody had moved. He stumbled half a pace, and she was already there with her hand on his collarbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:153:*Price: she has to see first. For the first minute and a half she's collecting, and she opens badly, twice today, because she hadn't finished reading him. Somebody she's never seen could take her early.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:175:"A minute and a half," said Cael. "Near enough. Both her bad openings fell inside it."

